<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-i2c">
    <title>I2C: A Two-Wire Bus, and a Display Driver</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            A four-digit seven-segment display has 34 LEDs in it.  Wiring one
            pin to each is not an option on a board whose Arduino headers carry
            22 pins in total, and one of those is already committed to the
            potentiometer.  The way out is a bus: two wires, shared by every
            device on them, with each device answering to an address.  That bus
            is <term>I2C</term> — Inter-Integrated Circuit, also written I<sup>2</sup>C
            and also called TWI, the Two-Wire Interface — and it is how a
            microcontroller talks to most of the small parts around it.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers two class days.  We'll use the display first
            and build its driver afterwards, which is the order this course
            usually takes.  The first day is the shorter one, and it starts
            with wiring: your own display is showing characters, driven from
            your own board over two wires, before the protocol is explained at
            all.  The rest of that day is spent working out what those two
            wires carried, with an oscilloscope on them.  The protocol is built in
            class, and written out afterwards in
            <xref ref="sec-i2c-reference"/>.
        </p>
        <p>
            The second day is everything underneath.  At the far end of the bus
            is the display's controller chip, the HT16K33, whose datasheet is
            four pages of command table and RAM map; at our end is the
            STM32C031C6's I2C1 peripheral, its two pins and its timing
            register.  By the end of that hour you have written the
            <term>device driver</term> that turns those datasheet pages into
            four functions the rest of your program can call.  That is the layer
            this course has been handing you all term — <c>uart.c</c>,
            <c>adc.c</c> — and this is the one you write yourself.  The
            chapter's one reading comes between the two days, and covers the
            display.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Identify the START, the address bits, the R/W bit, the ACK,
                    a data byte, and the STOP on a captured I2C trace, and say
                    which SDA edges are legal where.</li>
                <li>Say what makes I2C synchronous, what that saves compared
                    with UART, and what it costs.</li>
                <li>Name the five operations an I2C library has to provide, and
                    say why those five.</li>
                <li>Read the <c>I2C_TIMINGR</c> settings for a given peripheral
                    clock out of RM0490 §23.4.10, and say what PRESC is
                    for.</li>
                <li>Given a datasheet that states a device address ambiguously,
                    work out what to pass to <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c>.</li>
                <li>Explain why a four-digit display needs 14 wires and
                    time-division multiplexing, and what the backpack does with
                    them.</li>
                <li>Construct the HT16K33's initialization bytes from its
                    command table, and write a byte pattern that produces a
                    chosen character.</li>
                <li>Write a device-driver function that reaches the hardware
                    only through the I2C library, and write the whole display in
                    one transaction with <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c>.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-i2c-day9x">
        <title>Day 9X In-Class: Two Wires, and a Display That Works</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Today we'll start communicating with the seven-segment display
                in your kit.  This is a new peripheral, and we'll communicate
                with it using a new protocol.  We'll continue going to the
                reference manual for register information, and we'll continue
                using a whole mask to assign to a control register rather than
                compound-assigning one bit into it.
            </p>
            <p>
                We'll start by wiring the display to two of the Nucleo's pins,
                flash a program, and make sure the display lights up.
                Everything after that answers the question you then have —
                <em>how was that possible with just two wires?</em> — with an
                oscilloscope on those two wires, and then you change the bytes
                and put your own character on a digit of your choosing.
            </p>
            <p>
                The program we'll flash calls three functions out of a library
                we have not read, and we'll go through what they do tomorrow.
                We do this deliberately: we ran Blinky before we explained a
                single register, and using a thing before opening it up works
                well as long as we do open it up afterwards.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-motivation">
            <title>Part 1: How Many Pins Would You Need?</title>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-display-photo"/> is the display we are going to
                drive: four digits, each with seven segments and a decimal
                point, plus a colon between the middle two.  Before reading on,
                write down two numbers.  How many LEDs is that?  And if each one
                had its own pin on the STM32C031C6, how many pins would that
                take?
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-display-photo">
                <caption>The four-digit seven-segment display used in this
                course, mounted on an Adafruit HT16K33 backpack and shown with
                every segment lit.  Four digits of seven segments each, four
                decimal points and a two-dot colon come to 34 LEDs.  (The
                colon's two dots share a pin pair inside the display, so they
                always light together — 34 LEDs, 33 things you can switch.)  The four header pins along the top edge —
                labeled +, −, D and C on the board — are the only connections it
                needs.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/display_photo.png" width="60%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The count is 4 × 7 segments = 28, plus one decimal point per
                digit = 32, plus the colon's two dots = <term>34 LEDs</term>.
                One pin each is 34 pins.  The Nucleo's Arduino headers carry 22
                — D0 through D15 and A0 through A5 — and A0 is already the
                potentiometer's.  So the direct approach needs a dozen more pins
                than the headers have, before anything else on the board asks
                for one.
            </p>
            <p>
                So the display does not get 34 pins.  It gets four wires: power,
                ground, and the two wires of an I2C bus.  A separate chip on the
                small board underneath the display — a <term>backpack</term> —
                takes commands over those two wires and drives all 34 LEDs
                itself.  Wiring those four is what we do next.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-count" ref="fig-display-photo" room="yes">
    <p>The picture to the right shows our <term>seven-segment display,</term> mounted on a backpack controller</p>
    <ul>
        <li><term>How many LEDs are in this display?</term></li>
        <li>And if each one had its own pin on the STM32C031C6, <term>how many wires would that take?</term></li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Compare your count with your table group.</caption>
    <note>Equipment today: Nucleo, the display backpack, four jumper wires, and an AD2 per student — Part 3b assumes one instrument each. No components carried over from Day 9.
    NOTHING WAS ASSIGNED TO READ. The protocol is built from scratch in Parts 3a and 3b; do not open on "as you read last night". The written version goes into the Reference section, and the reading before Thursday covers the display and re-checks the protocol.
    Fifty minutes, and the parts as budgeted sum to 52, so two minutes have to come out somewhere. The two beats that cannot be cut are Part 2 (every display lit) and Part 5 (every student has written a digit's bytes). If it runs long, cut in this order: the Waveforms decode view at the end of Part 3c; then the paper address exercise in Part 4, which works as self-study because the book gives the answer under it; then Part 3a's third bullet.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-count-answer">
    <p><term>Each LED needs a pin and a ground wire, and the board does not have 34 pins to spare.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>4 digits &#215; 7 segments = 28, plus one decimal point per digit = 32, plus the colon's two dots = <term>34</term>.</li>
        <li>The Nucleo's Arduino headers carry <term>22</term> pins &#8212; more are available via the morpho connectors, but using up 34 pins is clearly not a viable solution.</li>
        <li>We'll wire the display with just <term>four wires</term>: power, ground, and the two wires of an I2C (Inter-Integrated Circuit) bus. A separate chip underneath the display &#8212; a <term>backpack</term> &#8212; takes commands from the STM32C0 over those two I2C wires via a special protocol and drives all 34 LEDs itself.</li>
        <li>We'll talk about the I2C protocol today and will examine how to talk to the backpack chip tomorrow.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-capture">
            <title>Part 2: Wire It, and Light It</title>
            <p>
                We'll wire the display with four wires, flash one program, and
                get characters onto it.  We'll bring the oscilloscope in
                afterwards, in <xref ref="subsec-day9x-protocol"/>, once there
                is something on the wires worth looking at.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Power first.</term>  The display's four connections are
                labeled on the board itself — <c>+</c>, <c>−</c>, <c>D</c> and
                <c>C</c> (<xref ref="fig-backpack-pins"/>).  The backpack's pin
                header plugs into the breadboard, so each of the four wires goes
                into the breadboard column that pin lands in.  <c>+</c> goes to
                the Nucleo's <term>3.3 V</term> pin, not the 5 V pin beside it.
                The reason is not the STM32's own tolerance — PB8 and PB9 are
                listed as 5 V tolerant in the datasheet's pin table.  It is that
                the backpack's pull-up resistors tie SDA (serial data) and SCL
                (serial clock) to <em>whatever you feed its <c>+</c> pin</em>,
                so its supply is also the voltage that ends up on the two
                signal wires, and this bus is built to sit at 3.3 V throughout.
            </p>
            <p>
                Check the four wires against
                <xref ref="fig-display-wiring"/> before you plug the Nucleo into
                USB.  We have not tested what happens when <c>+</c> and
                <c>−</c> are swapped, so check the four wires rather than
                finding out.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-backpack-pins">
                <caption>The display and its backpack, unpowered, with the four
                connections labeled on the board itself: + (supply), − (ground),
                D (SDA, the data line) and C (SCL, the clock line).  This is a
                bare board, photographed before its header was fitted; yours has
                a pin header soldered into those four holes and sits in the
                breadboard, so the wires go into the breadboard column each pin
                lands in rather than onto the board itself —
                <xref ref="fig-display-photo"/> and
                <xref ref="fig-display-wiring"/> both show it that way round.
                The pull-up resistors that hold both lines HIGH are already on
                this board, so no external resistors are needed.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/backpack_pins.png" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The two signal wires go to <term>PB9 (SDA — serial data)</term>
                and <term>PB8 (SCL — serial clock)</term>, which are D14 and
                D15 on the Arduino header.  We'll see tomorrow why those two
                pins and not others.
                <xref ref="fig-display-wiring"/> also shows the AD2 tapping the
                same two wires; leave that until
                <xref ref="subsec-day9x-protocol"/> and get the four wires in
                first.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-display-wiring">
                <caption>The full setup: Nucleo, display backpack and AD2 on one
                breadboard.  Four wires run from the backpack — + to the
                Nucleo's 3.3 V pin, − to GND, D to PB9 (SDA) and C to PB8 (SCL).
                The AD2's channel 1 (orange) and channel 2 (blue) scope leads tap
                the SCL and SDA rows on the breadboard, with the white-striped
                minus leads at ground.  Both signal lines are already pulled up
                on the backpack, so nothing else is needed to make the bus
                work.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/display_wiring_ad2.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The program is <c>helloDisplay.c</c>, and it is short because
                everything hard is inside the library you are given.  Read it
                before you flash it — not to understand every line, which is
                Thursday, but to see the shape.  Four calls set the display up
                and fill it, and then a loop keeps one short transaction going.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#include "i2c.h"

#define HT16K33_ADDR         0x70   // I2C address of our backpack controller
#define HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD   0x20   // system setup
#define HT16K33_OSC_ON       0x01
#define HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD  0x80   // display setup
#define HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON   0x01
#define HT16K33_BLINK_OFF    0x00
#define HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD   0xE0   // brightness, ORed with 0 to 15
#define HT16K33_ADDR_PTR     0x00   // display RAM address pointer

int main(void) {
    uint8_t data = 0b0;   // The hardware requires us to send some data, not important here.
    uint8_t display_buffer[10] = {
        0b01110110, 0x00,       // H
        0b00111111, 0x00,       // O
        0x00,       0x00,       // the colon, both dots off
        0b00111000, 0x00,       // L
        0b01110111, 0x00        // A
    };
    i2c1_init();
    // Wake the display up, then fill its display RAM
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD | HT16K33_OSC_ON);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON | HT16K33_BLINK_OFF);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD | 0x7);
    i2c1_memWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_ADDR_PTR, 10, display_buffer);

    // Keep one short transaction on the wire for the oscilloscope
    while(1) {
        i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, data);
        delay_ms(250);
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The three one-byte writes are commands to the HT16K33, and they
                are given here rather than derived, because deriving them needs
                the datasheet's command table and that is Thursday.  What is
                worth knowing now is the order.  The first of the three turns
                the chip's oscillator on, and until it is running the HT16K33
                accepts everything you send it and drives nothing at all — a
                display that has been sent the other two commands but not that
                one looks exactly like a display that is not plugged in.
            </p>
            <p>
                The fourth call, <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c>, sends the ten bytes of
                <c>display_buffer</c> in one go.  Ten bytes for four digits,
                because there are two per position and the colon has a position
                of its own; the odd-numbered bytes are 0 for a seven-segment
                digit, and Thursday explains why they still have to be sent.
            </p>
            <p>
                Then the loop, whose job is not to change anything.  The display
                is already showing what it is going to show; the loop just keeps
                something on the bus to look at.  I2C has no way to address a
                device without also sending it something, so the shortest thing
                you can put on the wire is a one-byte write, and the value does
                not matter here.  The <c>delay_ms(250)</c> makes that happen once
                every 250 ms — four transactions a second, slow enough that a
                single sweep catches exactly one, which is what
                <xref ref="subsec-day9x-protocol"/> needs.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-hello">
                <title>Wire the Display and Light It</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        <c>i2c.c</c>, <c>i2c.h</c> and <c>helloDisplay.c</c> are
                        on Canvas.  
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-hello-t2">
                    <statement><p>Put <c>i2c.c</c> and <c>i2c.h</c> in your
                    <c>mylib</c> folder, beside <c>uart.c</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-hello-t3">
                    <statement><p>Copy <c>TemplateProject</c> to a new project
                    named <c>SevenSegI2CFirstSteps</c>, put
                    <c>helloDisplay.c</c> in its <c>Src</c> folder, and build
                    and flash.  If the build fails, double-check that you have <c>i2c.c</c> and <c>i2c.h</c> in
                    your <c>mylib</c> folder.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-hello-t4">
                    <statement><p>Read what the display says.  Then, without
                    changing anything yet: which lines of the program put it
                    there, and which line is running now?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <em>Display still dark?</em>  Work down this list in order.
                The second entry is why the wires are worth checking carefully
                before you look at the code.
            </p>
            <ol>
                <li>The four wires.  Re-seat all of them against
                    <xref ref="fig-display-wiring"/>: <c>+</c> on the
                    <term>3.3 V</term> pin and not the 5 V pin beside it,
                    <c>−</c> on GND, <c>D</c> on PB9 and <c>C</c> on PB8.
                    <c>D</c> and <c>C</c> swapped is the one that is hardest to
                    see and easiest to do.</li>
                <li>A display that never answers also stops the program.
                    <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> waits for a flag the hardware does
                    not set when nobody acknowledges, so the very first call
                    never returns and none of the rest of the program runs.
                    From where you are sitting that looks the same as a display
                    that was never sent anything — which is why the wires come
                    first and the code does not.
                    <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-library"/> has the detail, and
                    Thursday makes it happen on purpose.</li>
                <li>Still stuck?  We're here to help!</li>
            </ol>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-backpack-pins" ref="fig-backpack-pins">
    <caption>The four labels to find on your own board: + (supply), &#8722; (ground), D (data, SDA) and C (clock, SCL). The header pins sit in the breadboard, so each wire goes into the column its pin lands in.</caption>
    <note>Up while they wire. This is the close-up of the labels; the next slide is where each one goes.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-wiring" ref="fig-display-wiring" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><c>+</c> to <term>3.3 V</term>, <c>&#8722;</c> to GND, <c>D</c> to <term>PB9 (SDA, serial data)</term>, <c>C</c> to <term>PB8 (SCL, serial clock)</term> &#8212; D14 and D15 on the Arduino header.</li>
        <li>I2C needs pull-up resistors on SCL and SDA. The backpack has them built in, so we wire none ourselves. We'll use the AD2 in a bit.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Check all four against this before plugging the USB cable in.</caption>
</slide>


            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-hello-code" room="compressed">
    <p>We'll flash <term>helloDisplay.c</term>. It sends the display three <term>commands</term> to wake it up, then ten bytes of data. Here are the names it uses for those, taken from the HT16K33's datasheet &#8212; we'll work out where the numbers come from tomorrow.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#define HT16K33_ADDR         0x70   // I2C address of our backpack controller
#define HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD   0x20   // system setup
#define HT16K33_OSC_ON       0x01
#define HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD  0x80   // display setup
#define HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON   0x01
#define HT16K33_BLINK_OFF    0x00
#define HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD   0xE0   // brightness, ORed with 0 to 15
#define HT16K33_ADDR_PTR     0x00   // display RAM address pointer
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Every I2C device has an <term>address</term>, and ours is <c>0x70</c>. The controller sends it first, so that the right device answers.</li>
        <li>The rest are the display chip's own commands. Turning its <term>oscillator</term> on is the one that matters: until it is running the chip accepts everything you send and drives nothing.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-hello-what" room="compressed">
    <p>And the program that uses them:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    uint8_t display_buffer[10] = {      // two bytes per digit position;
        0b01110110, 0x00,   // H        //   you write these yourself later
        0b00111111, 0x00,   // O
        0x00,       0x00,   // colon
        0b00111000, 0x00,   // L
        0b01110111, 0x00    // A
    };
    i2c1_init();                                  // set up the two pins
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD | HT16K33_OSC_ON);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON
                                                     | HT16K33_BLINK_OFF);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD | 0x7);
    i2c1_memWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_ADDR_PTR, 10, display_buffer);
    while(1) {
        i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, data);      // some data; value does not matter
        delay_ms(250);
    }
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Three one-byte writes send the commands; <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> sends all ten data bytes together. The loop <term>changes nothing</term> &#8212; the value sent does not matter. <c>delay_ms(250)</c> makes it <term>four transactions a second</term>, slow enough that one single sweep catches exactly one.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-ladder">
    <p><term>Display still dark?</term> Double-check:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>1 &#8212; <term>The four wires.</term> Re-seat all of them: <c>+</c> on the <term>3.3 V</term> pin and not the 5 V pin beside it, <c>&#8722;</c> on GND, <c>D</c> on PB9, <c>C</c> on PB8. <c>D</c> and <c>C</c> swapped is the hardest to see and the easiest to do.</li>
        <li>2 &#8212; <term>A display that never answers also stops the program.</term> <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> waits for a flag the hardware does not set when nobody acknowledges, so the <term>first</term> call never returns and none of the rest runs. So if the display is not properly connected then there is no way the program can run. This will look the same as a display that was never been sent anything &#8212; which is why you need to double-check your wires first.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Wires first. There is nothing in the code to change.</caption>
    <note>Stays up for all of Part 2. Checkpoint at minute 15: if the room is not mostly lit, put one working display on the document camera and move on — Part 3b's trace can be read from the projected capture.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-shared-clock">
            <title>Part 3a: How Did Two Wires Do That?</title>
            <p>
                Four characters are on your display and two wires put them
                there.  The rest of the hour is about what those two wires
                carried.  Nothing before today told you; we build it here, and
                <xref ref="subsec-i2c-physical-reality"/> has the written version
                for afterwards.
            </p>
            <p>
                Start with the one you have met before.  The UART sends a byte
                down a wire, and the receiver recovers the bits by starting a
                timer at the start bit and sampling on a schedule <em>both ends
                were configured for in advance</em> — the baud rate.  Nothing on
                the wire says where a bit ends, so get that rate wrong at either
                end and the frame comes out as garbage.
            </p>
            <p>
                I2C spends a wire to make that agreement go away
                (<xref ref="fig-uart-vs-i2c"/>).  The controller drives a
                <term>clock</term> line, <term>SCL</term>, alongside the data
                line, <term>SDA</term>, and every device on the bus reads SDA
                while SCL is HIGH.  No device has to know in advance how fast
                the bits will arrive, because the wire tells it.  That is what
                <term>synchronous</term> means here: the clock is shared, not
                reconstructed independently at each receiver.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-uart-vs-i2c">
                <caption>UART and I2C compared.  On the left, UART: a separate
                transmit and receive line between exactly two devices, no clock,
                and a baud rate both ends must be configured for in advance —
                but each direction has its own wire, so both can be in use at
                once.  On the right, I2C: one clock line (SCL) driven by the
                controller and one bidirectional data line (SDA), both held HIGH
                by pull-up resistors, with every device on the bus tapped onto
                the same two wires.  Adding a device to the UART arrangement
                costs another pair of pins; adding one to the I2C bus costs
                none.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="42% 52%" margins="3%">
                    <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/uart_point_to_point.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_bus_two_wires.svg"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The other half of the difference is that the two wires are
                <term>shared</term>.  Every I2C device is tapped onto the same
                SDA and the same SCL, which is what a <term>bus</term> is, and
                what it buys is that the wire count does not grow: a display, a
                temperature sensor and an accelerometer all connect to the same
                two wires.  Both lines idle HIGH, held there by pull-up
                resistors — the ones already on your backpack — and a device can
                only ever pull a line LOW.  To send a 1 it releases the line and
                lets the resistor bring it up.
            </p>
            <p>
                Only one device may talk at a time, so
                I2C is <term>half duplex</term> where UART is full duplex.  The
                whole bus runs at one speed, which has to be a speed every
                device on it can follow.  And because every device hears every
                bit, each one needs an <term>address</term> — which is what the
                controller sends first, every single time.  One device is in
                charge of all of this: the <term>controller</term>, our
                STM32C031C6, which generates the clock and starts every
                transaction.  Everything else is a <term>target</term>, and a
                target only speaks when spoken to.
            </p>
            <p>
                That address is the <c>HT16K33_ADDR</c> you have just flashed,
                and it is the first thing you will find on the wire.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-uart-i2c" ref="fig-uart-vs-i2c" stack="yes">
    <tabular halign="left" valign="top" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
        <col width="50%"/>
        <col width="50%"/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
            <cell>UART</cell>
            <cell>I2C</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>No serial clock. Start and stop bits are used to synchronize data recovery.</cell>
            <cell>Serial clock, generated by the bus controller, synchronizes data recovery.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Separate Tx and Rx lines. Full duplex (simultaneous transmit and receive) is possible.</cell>
            <cell>Common clock line (SCL) and <em>bidirectional</em> data line (SDA). Controller and peripheral take turns writing and reading.</cell>
        </row>
        <row>
            <cell>Each peripheral device has its own Tx/Rx pair.</cell>
            <cell>All peripherals use the same two-wire bus. Each has its own <em>bus address</em>, and every packet from the controller begins with the address of the device it is meant for.</cell>
        </row>
    </tabular>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-sharing-costs">
    <p>Two more things about sharing one pair of wires.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The whole bus runs at <term>one speed</term>, and it has to be a speed every device on it can follow. We use <term>100 kHz</term>, Standard mode &#8212; essentially every I2C device supports it, which is why it is the safe default.</li>
        <li>Our chip can go a lot faster: <term>400 kHz</term> (Fast mode) and <term>1 MHz</term> (Fast-mode Plus). The devices that need those are the ones moving a lot of data, an EEPROM being the usual example. A four-digit display sending ten bytes when the number changes is not one of them.</li>
        <li>The device in charge is the <term>controller</term> &#8212; our STM32C031C6 &#8212; which generates the clock and starts every transaction. Everything else is a <term>target</term>, and a target only speaks when spoken to.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Older documents, including RM0490's own I2C chapter, say "master" and "slave" for these two. Worth saying once: the hardware is the same, the words have changed, and they will meet the old pair in the manual.</note>
</slide>

        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-protocol">
            <title>Part 3b: One Transaction, Measured</title>
            <p>
                Two wires carried four characters to the display.  Here is what
                they carried, field by field
                (<xref ref="fig-i2c-message-frame"/>).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-message-frame">
                <caption>One I2C message, field by field.  It opens with a
                START condition and closes with a STOP, and between them the
                controller sends an <term>address frame</term> — seven address
                bits and the read/write bit — followed by as many
                <term>data frames</term> of eight bits as it needs.  Every frame
                is followed by a single ACK/NACK bit from whichever device is
                receiving.</caption>
                <image source="i2c_message_frame.svg" width="96%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <term>SCL and SDA idle high</term>, because of the pull-ups.
                The controller asserts control by pulling SDA low while SCL is
                high — the <term>START</term> — and SCL starts running.  It then
                transmits the device address, seven bits, followed by a 0 to
                command a write or a 1 to command a read.  It lets go of the data
                line, and the peripheral that recognizes its address
                acknowledges by sending a 0 back: the <term>ACK</term>.  Other
                devices on the bus do not respond, and if nobody responds at all
                the data line simply stays high, which is called a
                <term>NACK</term>.  Then data bytes, each one acknowledged the
                same way, and finally the <term>STOP</term>: the controller
                returns SDA to high while SCL is high, and both lines go back to
                idle.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-message-frame" ref="fig-i2c-message-frame" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><term>START:</term> the controller asserts control by pulling SDA low while SCL is high. SCL starts running.</li>
        <li><term>Address + Read/Write:</term> the controller transmits the device address (7 bits) followed by <term>0</term> to command a write, or <term>1</term> to command a read.</li>
        <li><term>ACK:</term> the controller lets go of the data line, and the peripheral that recognizes its address acknowledges by sending a <term>0</term> back. Other devices on the bus do not respond. If nobody responds the data line stays high, which is called a <term>NACK</term>.</li>
        <li><term>DATA:</term> the controller sends a byte and listens for the ACK or NACK, or receives a byte and replies with one.</li>
        <li><term>STOP:</term> the controller returns SDA to high while SCL is high, and both lines go back to idle.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <p>
                One question is left, and it is the one that makes the rest work.
                If SDA is the line carrying the bits, how does a device tell a
                START from an ordinary 1 going by?
            </p>
            <p>
                There is one rule, and everything else follows from it.
                <term>SDA is allowed to change only while SCL is LOW.</term>
                That is when no receiver is sampling it; while SCL is HIGH, SDA
                holds still and is read.  The protocol then takes the one thing
                that rule forbids and uses it to mark the edges of a
                transaction.  A <term>START condition</term> is SDA going HIGH to
                LOW <em>while SCL is HIGH</em>; a <term>STOP condition</term> is
                SDA going LOW to HIGH while SCL is HIGH.  Neither can occur
                during normal data transfer, so neither can be mistaken for a
                bit, and every device on the bus recognizes them immediately.
            </p>
            <p>
                One more piece, and it is the only moment in a transaction where
                the wire changes hands.  After the seven address bits and the
                R/W bit the controller lets go of SDA and keeps clocking, and
                the device whose address matched pulls SDA LOW for that ninth
                pulse.  That
                one-bit reply is the <term>ACK</term>.  SDA staying HIGH is a
                <term>NACK</term> — nobody claimed that address — and every data
                byte after the address is acknowledged the same way, by whichever
                side is receiving.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-start-stop">
                <caption>The two exceptions, on the wire.  SCL is above and SDA
                below, and the shaded bands mark the SCL LOW phases — the only
                times SDA is allowed to move.  At the left SDA falls while SCL is
                still HIGH, which is the START condition; at the right it rises
                while SCL is HIGH, which is the STOP.  Neither can happen during
                an ordinary data bit, which is what makes them
                unmistakable.</caption>
                <image source="i2c_start_stop.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-i2c-frame-diagram"/> is the whole of it in one
                picture: START, seven address bits, the R/W bit, ACK, a data
                byte, ACK, STOP.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-frame-diagram">
                <caption>One I2C write transaction.  SCL (top) begins clocking
                once the START condition has occurred.  SDA (bottom) shows the
                START — SDA falling while SCL is HIGH — then seven address bits
                and the R/W bit, which for address 0x70 and a write are
                1 1 1 0 0 0 0 followed by 0; then the ACK, with the addressed
                device pulling SDA LOW; then eight data bits, 0xAA = 10101010,
                most significant bit first; then a second ACK; and finally the
                STOP, SDA rising while SCL is HIGH.  Receivers sample SDA during
                the SCL HIGH phase, and SDA may change only while SCL is LOW —
                the START and STOP conditions being the two deliberate
                exceptions.</caption>
                <image source="i2c_transaction.svg" width="100%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The loop in <c>helloDisplay.c</c> is putting one of those on the
                wire four times a second, so all six parts of that diagram are
                on your breadboard right now.  Go and find them.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-scope-setup">
                <caption>The AD2 in oscilloscope mode, set up for this capture.
                The trigger is the part that matters: source <term>Channel 2</term>,
                the one on SDA; type <term>Edge</term>; condition
                <term>Falling</term>; level 1 V.  SDA idles HIGH and the first
                thing that happens in a transaction is SDA falling, so that
                setting catches the START.  The time base is 25 µs per division
                with the position at 100 µs, which fits the whole transaction on
                screen, and the two channels are given different offsets — −4 V
                on channel 1, 500 mV on channel 2 — so the traces sit one above
                the other instead of on top of each other.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/scope_ping_ack_1.png" width="92%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-capture">
                <title>Capture a Transaction and Mark It Up</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        We'll now use the AD2 to look at the oscilloscope trace of the I2C transaction that is happening
                        on SDA and SCL.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-capture-t3">
                    <statement><p>Double-check that your AD2 is wired as in picture 
                    <xref ref="fig-display-wiring"/>, in oscilloscope mode:
                    channel 1 (orange) on SCL, channel 2 (blue) on SDA, and both
                    minus leads — the ones with the white stripe — to
                    ground.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-capture-t3b">
                    <statement><p>With your <term>helloDisplay.c</term> running, take a <em>single sweep</em>.  Because of the 250 ms delay in the loop you are
                    catching one transaction out of four per second, so trigger
                    on a falling edge of the data line and let it find one.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-capture-t4">
                    <statement><p>On your own trace, mark all six: the START,
                    the seven address bits, the R/W bit, the ACK, the data byte,
                    and the STOP.  Write down the <term>eight</term> bit values
                    of that first byte — the seven address bits and the R/W bit
                    after them — in the order they go out.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-capture-t5">
                    <statement><p>How many clock pulses are in your capture, and
                    why that many?  Count them on the screen and verify with the protocol.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-capture-t6">
                    <statement><p>Two bytes went out in that transaction: the
                    address byte and the data byte, which for this loop is
                    <c>0x00</c>.  Which eight clock pulses would change if you
                    altered the data, and which if you altered the
                    address?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <em>Both channels sitting HIGH and nothing ever happening?</em>
                The bus is idle and correctly pulled up; the scope has not
                triggered.  In Waveforms' trigger panel, set the source to the
                channel on SDA and the condition to a falling edge, then arm a
                single sweep again.  Nothing there is a wiring problem, and the
                display staying lit is the proof.
            </p>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-framing" ref="fig-i2c-start-stop" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><term>SDA may change only while SCL is LOW.</term> While SCL is HIGH, SDA holds still and is read. So an SDA edge while SCL is HIGH cannot be a data bit &#8212; which is exactly what makes the START and the STOP unmistakable.</li>
        <li><term>START:</term> SDA goes HIGH to LOW while SCL is HIGH. <term>STOP:</term> SDA goes LOW to HIGH while SCL is HIGH. Every device on the bus recognizes both immediately.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>The two edges that fall outside the shaded bands are the only two SDA is allowed to make while SCL is HIGH — and they are the START and the STOP.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-scope-setup" ref="fig-scope-setup">
    <caption>The trigger is what matters: source Channel 2 (SDA), type Edge, condition Falling, level 1 V. SDA idles HIGH and falls at the START, so that setting catches it.</caption>
    <note>Up while they set the AD2 up. The offsets (-4 V and 500 mV) are only so the two traces do not sit on top of each other — say that, or somebody will think it is significant.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-protocol" ref="fig-i2c-frame-diagram">
    <caption>Six things to find: START, seven address bits, the R/W bit, the ACK, a data byte, STOP. Next you find all six on your own screen.</caption>
    <note>Name each field once — START, address, R/W, ACK, data, STOP — and do not walk it bit by bit; they are about to find every one of them on their own screen. The rule to say out loud is the one on the previous slide: SDA changes only while SCL is LOW, which is what leaves START and STOP free to mean something else.</note>
</slide>


        </subsection>


        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-debrief">
            <title>Part 3c: Reading Your Own Trace</title>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-i2c-scope-ack"/> is a capture of the transaction
                your loop is producing, and it should look like yours.  Eighteen
                clock pulses: nine for the address byte and its ACK, nine for the
                data byte and its ACK, with the START before them and the STOP
                after.  Keep the eight values you read off the address
                phase — <xref ref="subsec-day9x-addressing"/> asks you for
                them.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-scope-ack">
                <caption>One write to address 0x70, captured on the AD2 in
                oscilloscope mode: SDA in blue on channel 2 above, SCL in orange
                on channel 1 below, offset vertically so the two do not overlap.
                The whole transaction is eighteen clock pulses and takes under
                200 microseconds.  Reading left to right: both lines idle HIGH,
                SDA falls while SCL is still HIGH, SCL begins clocking, and the
                first blue pulse near 90 microseconds is the moment the display
                lets go of SDA after acknowledging.  This is what your own single
                sweep should look like.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/scope_ping_ack_2.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The ACK is the part worth slowing down on, because it is the
                only moment in the transaction where the wire changes hands.
                After the eighth bit the controller keeps clocking and lets go
                of SDA; the pull-up brings the line HIGH.  The display then pulls
                it LOW sometime during the clock's LOW phase, so that when SCL
                rises, SDA is stable and LOW.  On the next LOW phase the display
                lets go again and the controller takes over.  That second
                handover is the short HIGH excursion visible just after each ACK
                in <xref ref="fig-i2c-scope-ack"/> — and there is no matching one
                before the ACK, because the last address bit is a 0, so the line
                was already LOW and never had a chance to rise.  Either way it is
                harmless: nothing is read while SCL is LOW.
            </p>
            <p>
                A second route into the same trace, and it is worth taking even
                if your own capture was perfectly readable: the AD2 also has a
                logic-analyzer mode that decodes I2C for you.  It shows the
                address, the R/W bit, the ACK and the data as values rather than
                edges, which is a much faster way to answer <q>is this device
                answering at all?</q> when you are debugging.
                <xref ref="fig-waveforms-setup"/> is the setup.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-waveforms-setup">
                <caption>Getting the two signals on screen in Waveforms' logic
                mode.  The AD2's digital channels — the larger flying-lead
                bundle — go DIO0 (pink) to SDA, DIO1 (green) to SCL and black to
                ground.  Six steps: add DIO0 and DIO1 as signals, set the time
                base to 20 µs per division, click to make DIO0 falling the
                trigger, take a single sweep, use the cursors to measure the
                clock period, and drag the view so the start bit is at the
                left.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/waveforms_i2c_setup_1.png" width="92%"/>
            </figure>
            <figure xml:id="fig-waveforms-decode">
                <caption>Then the decoder itself, three more steps: add an I2C
                bus with DIO1 as clock and DIO0 as data, set DIO0 falling as the
                trigger again, and take another sweep if the old trace
                disappears.  The decoded row reads the transaction out directly
                — <c>h70 WR</c>, its <c>ACK</c>, the data byte, and its
                <c>ACK</c>.  (The data byte in this screenshot is <c>hBE</c>,
                because it was captured with a different value in the program;
                yours will differ.)</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/waveforms_i2c_setup_2.png" width="92%"/>
            </figure>
            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-ack" ref="fig-i2c-scope-ack">
    <caption>Find the START, the seven address bits, the R/W bit, the ACK on the ninth pulse, the data byte, and the STOP.</caption>
    <note>Eighteen pulses: nine for the address byte and its ACK, nine for the data byte and its ACK. The one to slow down on is the ninth — SDA is LOW because the display pulled it down, which is the only moment in the transaction where the wire changes hands.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-handover">
    <p>The ACK is the one moment in a transaction where <term>the wire changes hands</term>.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>The controller lets go of SDA and keeps clocking; the display pulls SDA down for that one pulse; then the display lets go and the controller takes it back.</li>
        <li>Both handovers happen while <term>SCL is LOW</term>, which is why they are harmless &#8212; nothing is read until the clock next rises. They are also usually too smooth to see: you may find a brief glitch on SDA around the ACK, and you may equally well find nothing at all.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>


            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-decode" ref="fig-waveforms-setup">
    <caption>Logic mode, not oscilloscope mode: add DIO0 and DIO1, time base 20 µs/div, DIO0 falling as the trigger, single sweep.</caption>
    <note>Offer this to the whole room, not only to fast finishers. Reading the raw trace stays the skill; this is the instrument you reach for when the question is "is this device answering at all?". The cursors on two SCL rising edges should read 10 µs.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-decode-2" ref="fig-waveforms-decode">
    <caption>Then add an I2C bus — DIO1 as clock, DIO0 as data — and the edges become values: h70 WR, ACK, the data byte, ACK. You can read the address off it without counting a single bit.</caption>
    <note>The data byte here is hBE because this capture was taken with a different value in the program; theirs will read differently.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>





        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-addressing">
            <title>Part 4: The Number on the Wire</title>
            <p>
                Go back to the eight bits you wrote down off your own trace,
                before reading on.  Your program says <c>0x70</c>.  Read your
                eight values as a byte and say what number you got.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-on-the-wire">
                <title>Which Number Is on the Wire?</title>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-wire-t1">
                    <statement><p>Write <c>0x70</c> in binary, eight bits.  Now
                    write the eight bits you read off your trace.  They are not
                    the same number — by how much, and in which
                    direction?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-wire-t2">
                    <statement><p>The program passed 0x70 and the wire carries
                    something else, so something between the two moved the bits.
                    Where could that have happened, given that the only thing
                    between your <c>#define</c> and the wire is
                    <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c>?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-wire-t3">
                    <statement><p>Eight bits went out but the address is seven.
                    Which one is left over, and — thinking about what a
                    transaction has to say before any data moves — what is it
                    likely to be for?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-scope-decoded">
                <caption>The same capture with the address phase read off it.
                The <term>Start condition</term> is where SDA falls while SCL is
                still HIGH.  The eight bits that follow are
                1 1 1 0 0 0 0 and then 0: seven address bits, which are 0x70,
                and then the bit circled in red — the R/W bit, 0 for a write.
                The device's <c>Ack</c> comes on the pulse after that.  Read all
                eight as a single byte and you get 0xE0, which is the number on
                the wire rather than the number in the program.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/scope_ping_ack_4.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The byte on the wire is <c>0xE0</c>.  0x70 is a
                <em>seven-bit</em> number, 0b1110000, and what goes onto SDA is
                those seven bits followed by one more: the R/W bit, 0 to write
                to the device and 1 to read from it.  Read all eight as a byte
                and you get 0xE0.  Nothing is wrong — the address is in the top
                seven bits and the bottom bit is the direction — and
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> did the shift on your behalf, which is a
                line of it you will read on Thursday.
            </p>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-ht16k33-addr-frame"/> is the HT16K33
                datasheet's own drawing of a command transaction, and the
                address field in it is written <c>1 1 1 0 A2 A1 A0</c>: four
                fixed bits and three that are set by solder pads on the board, so
                that up to eight of these displays can share one bus.  Ours has
                all three at 0, which makes the 7-bit address 0b1110000 —
                <c>0x70</c>.  The next box along is the write bit, and after it
                the ACK.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ht16k33-addr-frame">
                <caption>The HT16K33 datasheet's diagram of a command write.  The
                first field, the device address, is
                1 1 1 0 A2 A1 A0 — the top four bits are fixed by the chip and
                A2, A1 and A0 are selectable on the board, so eight of these can
                share a bus.  With all three at 0 the 7-bit address is 0b1110000,
                which is 0x70.  The next bit is the direction, 0 for write; then
                the device's ACK; then the command byte, D15 down to D8; then a
                second ACK and the STOP.  Note that the address occupies seven
                bit positions and the direction bit is separate — the datasheet
                is being explicit about something many are not.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/ht16k33_command_transaction.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                One caution, because it costs people time.  Datasheets are not
                consistent about which of the two numbers they print: some give
                the 7-bit address and some give it already shifted left by one.
                A 7-bit address is at most <c>0x7F</c>, so any address quoted
                above that — <c>0xE0</c>, say — cannot be the 7-bit form and
                must be the shifted one.  Shift it back before you
                <c>#define</c> it.  Get that backwards and the device never
                answers, which looks exactly like a wiring fault.  It has
                happened to this course, once.
            </p>
            <p>
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> and its relatives take the
                <term>7-bit</term> address and do the shift themselves, so
                whatever a datasheet gives you, what you <c>#define</c> is the
                7-bit form.
            </p>
            <p>
                Two things to take from a failed transaction.  The first is that
                a NACK is not an error message from the device — it is the
                <em>absence</em> of an answer, and it looks exactly the same
                whether the address is wrong, the device has no power, or the
                SDA wire is not connected.  The second is that the controller
                kept clocking anyway.  It sent all nine pulses and the STOP
                without knowing, until the ninth, whether anyone was there at
                all.  Thursday has you produce this capture yourself, and one
                other that is more interesting than it.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-decoded" ref="fig-i2c-scope-decoded">
    <caption>Your own trace, with the address phase read off it: 1 1 1 0 0 0 0 is the 7-bit address 0x70, and the bit circled in red is the R/W bit — 0 for a write. Then the ACK.</caption>
    <note>Show this after they have committed their eight values. The red circle is the eighth bit, not the ACK — the ACK is the green label after it.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-address-shift">
    <p><term>The address you write and the byte on the wire are not the same number.</term> This is the number you just read off your own trace.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
0x70  is a SEVEN-bit address:      1 1 1 0 0 0 0

on the wire:      1 1 1 0 0 0 0    0
                  \___address___/  R/W:  0 = write, 1 = read
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Read those eight bits as a byte and you get <term>0xE0</term>, not 0x70. Nothing is wrong: the address is in the top seven bits and the bottom bit is the direction.</li>
        <li><c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> did the shift for you. On Thursday we'll look at <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> more carefully and see where this shift happens.</li>
        <li>Caution: datasheets are not consistent about which of the two numbers they provide: the one that's already shifted to the left by one of the pure address.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>




        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-one-digit">
            <title>Part 5: Write One Digit Yourself</title>
            <p>
                The display is showing four characters that somebody else chose.
                Now we'll put your own character there.  The only thing you need
                first is what a byte of <c>display_buffer</c> means.
            </p>
            <p>
                Each digit is seven bar-shaped LEDs and a round one, and they
                have names: <c>a</c> at the top, then <c>b</c> and <c>c</c> down
                the right, <c>d</c> along the bottom, <c>e</c> and <c>f</c> up
                the left, <c>g</c> across the middle, and <c>DP</c> for the
                decimal point (<xref ref="fig-segment-map"/>, which prints them
                as capitals).  Those are the names the datasheet uses, and they are the order the bits are
                in: bit 0 is <c>a</c>, bit 1 is <c>b</c>, on up to bit 6 for
                <c>g</c>, and bit 7 is the decimal point.  Written as a binary
                literal a digit's byte therefore reads
                <c>0b</c> DP <c>g f e d c b a</c>, most significant bit first —
                which is why the <c>H</c> in the program is
                <c>0b01110110</c>: bits 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6, so <c>b c e f g</c>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-segment-map">
                <caption>The four digits with every segment drawn, and the
                segment names on the first one: <c>a</c> across the top, <c>b</c>
                and <c>c</c> down the right side, <c>d</c> across the bottom,
                <c>e</c> and <c>f</c> up the left side, <c>g</c> across the
                middle, and <c>DP</c> for the decimal point.  The colon between
                digits 2 and 3 is two more LEDs, called <c>D5</c> and <c>D6</c>
                here.  Four digits of eight, plus the colon's two, is 34
                LEDs.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/seven_seg_segment_map.png" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Ten bytes, two per position.  <c>display_buffer[0]</c> is the
                first digit, <c>[2]</c> the second, <c>[4]</c> the colon,
                <c>[6]</c> the third and <c>[8]</c> the fourth; the odd-numbered
                entries are the ones that stay 0, and Thursday is where you find
                out why they cannot simply be left out.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-your-digit">
                <title>Put Your Own Character on a Digit</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        In <c>helloDisplay.c</c>, edit
                        <c>display_buffer</c> and nothing else.  Derive each
                        byte from <xref ref="fig-segment-map"/> rather than
                        guessing it, and write it down in binary before you
                        build.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-digit-t1">
                    <statement><p>Work out the byte for an <c>E</c>: which
                    segments are lit, which bits are those, and what is the
                    binary literal?  Put it in <c>display_buffer[0]</c>, build,
                    flash, and check the first digit against what you
                    predicted.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-digit-t2">
                    <statement><p>Move the <c>E</c> around:  Change one line so the <c>E</c>
                    appears on the second digit instead, then the third, then
                    the fourth.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-digit-t3">
                    <statement><p>Add a decimal point after that digit, by
                    changing one bit of one byte.  Work out which bit before you set
                    it.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-digit-t4">
                    <statement><p>Predict, then try: put your <c>E</c> byte into
                    <c>display_buffer[1]</c> — an odd entry — instead of
                    <c>[0]</c>.  What do you expect to see, and what do you
                    actually get?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-digit-t5">
                    <statement><p>Done early?  Spell a four-character word.  Not
                    every letter is possible with seven segments; part of the
                    exercise is finding out which ones are.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                An <c>E</c> is <c>a</c>, <c>d</c>, <c>e</c>, <c>f</c> and
                <c>g</c> — bits 0, 3, 4, 5 and 6 — so the byte is
                <c>0b01111001</c>.  A decimal point after the second digit means
                setting bit 7 of <c>display_buffer[2]</c>.  And the odd entry:
                the display RAM does have an address there and the byte does
                land in it, but on a seven-segment digit nothing is wired to the
                LEDs it would drive, so the character disappears rather than
                appearing somewhere unexpected.  Thursday's RAM map says why.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-segment-map" ref="fig-segment-map" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Each digit is seven bar-shaped LEDs and a round one, and they have names: <c>a</c> across the top, <c>b</c> and <c>c</c> down the right, <c>d</c> along the bottom, <c>e</c> and <c>f</c> up the left, <c>g</c> across the middle, <c>DP</c> for the decimal point. (The drawing prints them as capitals; we write them lowercase, as the datasheet does.)</li>
        <li>Those names are the <term>order the bits are in</term>: bit 0 is <c>a</c>, up to bit 6 for <c>g</c>, and bit 7 is the decimal point. So a binary literal reads <c>0b</c> DP g f e d c b a, most significant first.</li>
        <li>Which is why the <c>H</c> in the program is <c>0b01110110</c>: bits 1, 2, 4, 5, 6 &#8212; segments <c>b c e f g</c>.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Read the bit numbering off the first digit, then check it against the H you already have lit.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-buffer-map" room="yes">
    <p><term>Ten bytes, two per position.</term> The digits are the even entries; the odd ones stay 0 (we'll see on Thursday why).</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    display_buffer[0]   digit 1        display_buffer[1]   0
    display_buffer[2]   digit 2        display_buffer[3]   0
    display_buffer[4]   the colon      display_buffer[5]   0
    display_buffer[6]   digit 3        display_buffer[7]   0
    display_buffer[8]   digit 4        display_buffer[9]   0
]]></code></program>
    <caption>Keep this up while you work. Everything you change today is one entry of this array.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-digit-answer">
    <p><term>The bytes.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    E  =  a d e f g   =  bits 0, 3, 4, 5, 6   =  0b01111001

    display_buffer[0] = 0b01111001;   // E on the first digit
    display_buffer[2] = 0b01111001;   // ... on the second
    display_buffer[2] = 0b11111001;   // ... and with its decimal point: bit 7
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The odd entry: the display RAM does have an address there and the byte does land in it, but on a seven-segment digit nothing is wired to the LEDs it would drive &#8212; so the character <term>disappears</term> rather than turning up somewhere unexpected.</li>
        <li>Thursday's RAM map is what makes that predictable rather than surprising.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Instructor copy. Let them derive the E; this is for checking, and for anyone who has to be caught up.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-next">
            <title>Part 6: Before Next Class</title>
            <p>
                The only thing due tonight is Tuesday's timer program, and
                we'll open tomorrow by reviewing it.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Leave the display wired.</term>  Thursday starts by
                flashing <c>helloDisplay.c</c> again to prove the bus survived
                the night, and then spends the rest of a much longer hour on
                everything that was given to you today: the chip at the far end
                of the two wires — its command table and its display RAM — and
                the peripheral at our end, its two pins and its timing register.
                Then you write the device driver yourself.
            </p>
            <p>
                Two things were given to you today rather than explained, both
                deliberately.  You called <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> and
                <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> without knowing what either does, and we'll
                open both tomorrow.  And you sent three command bytes that were
                handed to you rather than derived; we'll take those out of the
                datasheet tomorrow as well.
            </p>

        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-display-before-class">
        <title>Before Class: The Display, and the Bus You Have Used</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                This is the chapter's one reading, and it comes after a class
                rather than before one.  It has two jobs.  The first is the
                display itself — the thing at the far end of the two wires,
                which the next class takes apart and writes a driver for, and
                which nothing so far has explained.  That is what the two
                subsections below are.
            </p>
            <p>
                The second is to consolidate the protocol you worked out on an
                oscilloscope in class.  There is nothing new in it, and you do
                not need to read it again if it stuck — but the reading
                questions cover it as well as the display, because the next
                class opens the peripheral and the library that produce it, and
                both are much harder to follow if START, ACK and STOP are still
                vague.  The written version is at the top of the
                <xref ref="sec-i2c-reference"/> section: four short
                subsections, beginning with
                <xref ref="subsec-i2c-physical-reality"/>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-display-sharing">
            <title>34 LEDs, and Not 34 Wires</title>
            <p>
                An LED lights when current flows through it in one direction, so
                controlling one LED means controlling one path from a positive
                supply, through the LED, to ground.  Thirty-four LEDs with
                thirty-four separate paths is thirty-four wires plus a common
                return, and the display in your kit does not have that many
                pins.  It has fourteen.
            </p>
            <p>
                The way that works is a trick you will see again in every
                display you ever meet, and it has two halves.  The first half is
                <term>sharing</term>: the LEDs are wired in a grid rather than
                individually, so that a single wire reaches many of them at once
                and the LED that lights is the one where an energized row crosses
                an energized column.  A grid of <m>r</m> rows and <m>c</m>
                columns reaches <m>r \times c</m> LEDs with <m>r + c</m> wires,
                and that gap is the whole saving.
            </p>
            <p>
                The second half is <term>time</term>.  A grid can only light one
                row at a time without lighting things you did not ask for, so
                the display shows one row, then the next, then the next, cycling
                through all of them fast enough that your eye cannot follow.
                Persistence of vision does the rest: what you see is a steady
                image, even though at every instant most of the display is dark.
                This is <term>time-division multiplexing</term>, and it is why a
                display like this is never really <q>set and forget</q> — the
                image only exists because something is rewriting it, thousands
                of times a second, forever.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-display-backpack">
            <title>What the Backpack Is For</title>
            <p>
                That <q>something</q> could be your program.  The STM32C031C6
                is more than fast enough to drive fourteen pins in the right
                pattern, and people do exactly this.  The cost is that it never
                stops: the moment your program does something else for a few
                milliseconds, the display visibly flickers or goes dark.  Every
                other thing you might want the processor to do has to be fitted
                around a refresh loop.
            </p>
            <p>
                The alternative is to give the job to a chip that does nothing
                else.  The small board the display is soldered to — the
                <term>backpack</term> — carries an HT16K33, a controller whose
                entire purpose is to drive an LED matrix.  It has a RAM inside
                it holding which LEDs should be on, its own oscillator, and the
                logic to sweep the grid continuously from that RAM.  It also has
                an I2C interface, and that is the only part of it your program
                talks to.
            </p>
            <p>
                So the division of labor is: your program says which LEDs should
                be on, once, whenever it changes its mind; the HT16K33 keeps them
                on until told otherwise.  Two wires carry the first half, and
                nothing carries the second half because it never leaves the
                backpack.  In class we will read the HT16K33's datasheet to find
                out what <q>says which LEDs should be on</q> means in bytes, and
                write the code that says it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-i2c-how-it-works">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-i2c-bus-listeners">
                <statement><p>An I2C bus has four devices connected to the same
                SCL and SDA lines.  The controller sends an address byte of 0x70.
                How many of the four examine those address bits?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>All four examine the address; only the one at
                        address 0x70 responds with ACK.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  Because all devices share SDA, every device
                        sees every bit.  Each one compares the address it hears against
                        its own; only the matching device pulls SDA LOW for the ACK.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Only the device at address 0x70 sees the bits;
                        the others are unaware of the transaction.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>All devices are physically connected to SDA and SCL —
                        they all receive every bit.  There is no mechanism to hide bits
                        from particular devices, and none is needed: a device that does not
                        recognize the address simply stays quiet.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Two devices are selected at once; the
                        one with the lower address wins.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>On a correctly configured bus each device has a
                        distinct address, so only one can match a given address byte.
                        Parts that ship with the same default address usually have pins
                        or solder pads for changing a few of its bits — which is exactly
                        what you would use to avoid this.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The controller pre-selects one device before sending
                        the address byte, so only that device responds.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>There is no such pre-selection step.  The address
                        byte itself is the selection, and every device evaluates it in
                        parallel.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-i2c-start-unique">
                <statement><p>What makes a START condition impossible to confuse with
                a normal data bit?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>SDA changes while SCL is HIGH; data bits may
                        change only while SCL is LOW.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The normal rule is that SDA holds still
                        during the SCL HIGH phase, because that is when the receiver reads
                        it.  START and STOP deliberately break that rule, so an SDA edge
                        while SCL is HIGH cannot be a bit value.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The controller holds SCL LOW for twice as long
                        before the START condition.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>SCL timing does not change at the START.  What
                        distinguishes it is SDA changing while SCL is HIGH, not anything
                        about SCL itself.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The controller transmits a reserved 0xFF byte
                        immediately before the START condition.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>No such preamble exists.  START is identified entirely
                        by the relationship between SDA and SCL.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>SDA is held LOW for at least eight SCL cycles
                        before data transmission begins.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>SDA falls once, for one transition, at the START.
                        The key is <em>when</em> SDA changes — while SCL is HIGH — not how
                        long it stays LOW.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-i2c-nack-meaning">
                <statement><p>After sending the 7-bit address and the R/W bit, the
                controller releases SDA and watches it during the ninth SCL pulse.
                SDA is HIGH during that pulse.  What does this tell the
                controller?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>No device recognized the address (NACK) — the
                        controller should issue a STOP rather than continue.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  A matching device would pull SDA LOW.  If SDA
                        stays HIGH, nobody claimed the address, and the controller ends the
                        transaction with a STOP to free the bus.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The transaction completed successfully and the
                        data was received.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A successful address phase shows SDA LOW — the device
                        pulling it down.  SDA HIGH after the address byte means the
                        opposite: no device answered.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>The device is busy and will pull SDA LOW on the
                        next SCL pulse, once it is ready.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>A device that needs more time holds <em>SCL</em> LOW,
                        not SDA — that is called clock stretching, and the ACK still has to
                        arrive in its own clock pulse.  SDA HIGH during the ninth pulse is
                        a NACK.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Two devices share the same address and are
                        colliding on SDA, producing an indeterminate voltage.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>If two devices shared an address and both acknowledged,
                        both would pull SDA LOW and the line would read LOW.  Open-drain
                        signaling has no indeterminate state.  SDA HIGH means nobody
                        responded at all.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>


        <reading-questions xml:id="rq-display-hardware">
            <exercise xml:id="rq-display-why-multiplex">
                <statement><p>A four-digit display has 34 LEDs and 14 pins.  At
                any single instant, roughly how many of those LEDs can be
                lit?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Only the ones in one row of the grid — a
                        fraction of the display.  The rest are dark until their
                        turn comes round.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct, and it is the price of the wire
                        saving.  A grid can energize one row at a time without
                        lighting LEDs in other rows, so the display is swept row
                        by row.  What makes it look continuous is that the sweep
                        is much faster than the eye.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>All 34, because each LED has its own
                        controllable path through the grid.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>If each LED had its own path there would be
                        no saving and no need for a grid — that arrangement is
                        exactly the 34 wires the grid exists to avoid.  In a grid
                        the wires are shared, so lighting an arbitrary set of
                        LEDs at one instant is not possible.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Exactly one, because only one wire can be
                        energized at a time.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Closer, but too pessimistic.  One row's
                        worth can be lit together — every LED where the energized
                        row meets an energized column — which is why a whole
                        digit can be shown at once.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>All 34, but at a lower brightness, because
                        the current is shared between them.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Brightness does drop as the number of rows
                        goes up, because each row is only lit for its share of
                        the time.  But that is a consequence of the sweeping, not
                        an alternative to it: at any one instant the LEDs outside
                        the current row are fully off.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-display-backpack-job">
                <statement><p>What would your program have to do differently if
                the display had no HT16K33 on it — if the fourteen wires came
                straight to the STM32C031C6?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>It would have to keep sweeping the grid
                        continuously, forever, fast enough that the display does
                        not flicker — on top of whatever else it was
                        doing.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct.  The image exists only while
                        something is refreshing it.  That is the job the backpack
                        takes off your program, and it is why the backpack is
                        worth two wires and a datasheet.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It would have to send the display data
                        fourteen times instead of once.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The number of wires is not the number of
                        sends.  What changes is not how much data you send but
                        that you can never stop sending it — the refresh has to
                        keep running.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>Nothing would change in the program; only
                        the wiring would.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The wiring is the smaller half.  Without a
                        controller, generating the multiplexed sweep becomes the
                        program's job, and it is a job with a hard real-time
                        deadline that never ends.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It would need a faster clock, because I2C
                        at 100 kHz is too slow to refresh a
                        display.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>The bus speed is not the constraint: with a
                        backpack the display data is sent once, when it changes.
                        Without one, there is no bus at all — the fourteen wires
                        are driven directly, and the constraint is that the
                        driving never stops.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>

            <exercise xml:id="rq-display-grid-count">
                <statement><p>A grid needs one wire per row and one per column.
                Which arrangement reaches 34 LEDs with the fewest
                wires?</p></statement>
                <choices randomize="yes">
                    <choice correct="yes">
                        <statement><p>Something close to square — around 6 by 6 —
                        because for a fixed number of LEDs the sum of rows and
                        columns is smallest when the two are
                        similar.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Correct in principle.  1 by 34 needs 35
                        wires; 2 by 17 needs 19; 6 by 6 needs 12.  The real
                        display is not free to choose, because the grid has to
                        match how the digits are physically wired — which is what
                        we look at in class.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>One row of 34, because a single row needs
                        only one row wire.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>One row wire, but 34 column wires — 35 in
                        total, which is worse than no grid at all.  The saving
                        comes from balancing the two, not from minimizing one of
                        them.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>34 rows of one, so that each LED can be
                        addressed on its own.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>That is the individually wired arrangement
                        again, with the same 35 wires.  It does give you
                        arbitrary control at any instant, which is exactly what
                        you trade away for the wire saving.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                    <choice>
                        <statement><p>It makes no difference; any grid holding 34
                        LEDs needs the same number of wires.</p></statement>
                        <feedback><p>Compare 1 by 34 (35 wires) with 6 by 6 (12).
                        The number of LEDs is the product; the number of wires is
                        the sum, and the two behave very
                        differently.</p></feedback>
                    </choice>
                </choices>
            </exercise>
        </reading-questions>
    </section>

        <!--
        CANVAS QUIZ: "Before class — I2C protocol concepts"
        (Due 1 hour before the SECOND of the two class days; auto-graded multiple
        choice; low stakes.)  Same concepts as the reading questions above,
        different scenarios, so the book is not an answer key.

        NOTE: this quiz used to sit before the x-hour, when the protocol was a
        pre-class reading.  There is no reading before an x-hour, so the
        protocol is now taught in class on that day and this quiz checks it
        afterwards, alongside the display reading.

        Q1. Four I2C devices are on one bus at addresses 0x20, 0x48, 0x70 and 0x77.
            The controller sends an address byte for 0x48. Which statement is true?
            A) Only the device at 0x48 drives SDA LOW for the ACK.   <- CORRECT
            B) All four devices drive SDA LOW simultaneously.
            C) The devices at 0x48 and 0x49 both respond because they differ by one bit.
            D) No device responds until the controller first sends a "wake" byte.

        Q2. A logic analyzer shows: START; eight bits of address + R/W; SDA HIGH
            during the ninth SCL pulse; STOP. All devices are powered and the
            pull-ups are present. Most likely cause?
            A) The controller sent the wrong I2C address.   <- CORRECT
            B) The SCL frequency is higher than the device supports.
            C) The controller should have sent a repeated START.
            D) The controller forgot the R/W bit before the ACK slot.

        Q3. You add a third device (address 0x1D) to a bus that already has two
            working devices at 100 kHz. What must change on the existing two?
            A) Nothing.   <- CORRECT
            B) Each must be told the new bus speed.
            C) Their addresses must be incremented to avoid a conflict.
            D) The pull-ups must be replaced with lower-value resistors.

        Q4. A device uses open-drain signaling on SDA. What does that mean?
            A) It can pull SDA LOW but cannot drive it HIGH; a pull-up returns the
               line HIGH when nobody holds it down.   <- CORRECT
            B) It drives SDA HIGH and LOW at full current, like a push-pull GPIO.
            C) SDA is always LOW unless the controller raises it with a command.
            D) It means the device can only receive, never transmit.

        Q5. A controller writes 3 data bytes. The device ACKs bytes 1 and 2 and
            NACKs byte 3. What should the controller do next?
            A) Issue a STOP condition to end the transaction.   <- CORRECT
            B) Re-transmit byte 3 on the next SCL pulse.
            C) Issue a repeated START and re-send all three bytes.
            D) Keep sending; a NACK on one byte is not fatal.
        -->
    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-i2c-day10">
        <title>Day 10 In-Class: The HT16K33 and Its Device Driver</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Yesterday you used a bus.  Today you find out what you were
                using.  The wiring is the wiring you left in place and the
                program is the program you flashed; what is new is everything
                that was handed to you — the chip at the far end of the two
                wires, the peripheral at our end, and the library call in
                between.
            </p>
            <p>
                It is a long session, and it goes in that order: the display and
                its controller first, because that is where yesterday's
                unanswered questions are; then the STM32C031C6's own I2C1
                peripheral, its two pins and its timing register; then the five
                operations an I2C library has to provide, and
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> opened up.
            </p>
            <p>
                By the end of the hour your own display shows <c>ES.28</c>,
                written out of a ten-byte buffer by a
                <term>device driver</term> you wrote.  That is the layer this
                course has been handing you all term as <c>uart.c</c> and
                <c>adc.c</c>.  Lab 5 asks for that function, and this is where
                it comes from.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-homework">
            <title>Part 1: Homework Review, and Two AI Solutions</title>
            <p>
                Tuesday's homework was <c>counterResetButtonIntTimer.c</c>: the
                button-reset counter with <c>delay_ms()</c> replaced by a
                one-second timer interrupt, so that the program has two
                interrupt sources and blocks on neither.  If yours is not
                running, take the working copy from Canvas before we start — the
                next twenty minutes are about reading code, not about whose
                built.
            </p>
            <p>
                Both of the programs below are answers to that assignment,
                produced by asking an AI assistant to do it.  Neither is
                nonsense; both would compile.  Read one of them closely.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* counterResetButtonIntTimer.c -- ChatGPT */
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#define MAXCOUNT 100
void pb4_exti_init(void);
void tim14_init(void);
volatile int buttonPushed;
volatile int timerElapsed;

int main(void) {
  int counter = 0;
  uart2_init();
  printf("Counter demo\r\n");
  printf("Hex \tDecimal\r\n");
  pb4_exti_init();
  tim14_init();
  buttonPushed = 0;
  timerElapsed = 0;
  while (1) {
    if (timerElapsed) {
      timerElapsed = 0;
      printf("%x\t%d\r\n", counter, counter);
      if (buttonPushed) {
        counter = 0;
        buttonPushed = 0;
      } else if (counter == MAXCOUNT - 1) {
        counter = 0;
      } else {
        counter++;
      }
    }
  }
  return 1;
}

void TIM14_IRQHandler(void) {
    if (TIM14->SR & TIM_SR_UIF) {
        TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;      // Clear PR flag
        timerElapsed = 1;
    }
}

void tim14_init(void) {
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    TIM14->PSC = 48000 - 1;
    TIM14->ARR = 1000 - 1;
    TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;
    TIM14->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;
    NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn);
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}

void EXTI4_15_IRQHandler(void){
    // unchanged from our code
}

void pb4_exti_init(void) {
    // unchanged from our code
}
]]></code></program>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* counterResetButtonIntTimer.c -- Gemini */
#include <stdio.h>
#include "ES28.h"
#include "uart.h"
#define MAXCOUNT 100
void pb4_exti_init(void);
void tim14_init(void);
volatile int buttonPushed;

int main(void) {
  int counter = 0;
  // Initialize peripherals
  uart2_init();
  pb4_exti_init();
  tim14_init();
  buttonPushed = 0;
  printf("Counter ...\r\n");
  printf("Hex \tDecimal\r\n");
  while(1) {
    if (TIM14->SR & TIM_SR_UIF) {
      TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;        // Clear PR flag
      printf("%x\t%d\r\n", counter, counter);
      // Logic for counting and button reset
      if (buttonPushed) {
        counter = 0;
        buttonPushed = 0;
      } else if (counter >= MAXCOUNT - 1) {
        counter = 0;
      } else {
        counter++;
      }
    }
  }
  return 1;
}

void tim14_init(void) {
    RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;
    TIM14->PSC = 16000 - 1;
    TIM14->ARR = 1000 - 1;
    TIM14->EGR |= TIM_EGR_UG;
    TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;
}

void EXTI4_15_IRQHandler(void){
    // unchanged from our code
}

void pb4_exti_init(void) {
    // unchanged from our code
}
]]></code></program>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-ai-review">
                <title>Would You Revise Your Solution?</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Pick one of the two.  Answer the first three questions in
                        writing, on your own, before anybody talks.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-ai-t0">
                    <statement><p>First, a measurement, because the rest of this
                    depends on it.  Your own program prints once a second: time
                    ten prints against a stopwatch and write down what you got.
                    Then write down the <c>PSC</c> and <c>ARR</c> your program
                    uses.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-ai-t1">
                    <statement><p>What does it do better than yours?  There is
                    something.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-ai-t2">
                    <statement><p>Find at least two things you would change, and
                    for each one say what would go wrong on a real board — not
                    that it is bad style, but what a user would see.  For at
                    least one of them, say what would happen on <em>your</em>
                    board, using the interval you just timed and the
                    <c>PSC</c> and <c>ARR</c> you just wrote
                    down.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-ai-t3">
                    <statement><p>Having read it: would you revise your own
                    solution?  One sentence, either way.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-ai-t4">
                    <statement><p>Now compare with the others at your table, and
                    put together the list of everything the group found.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                What is there to find.  <term>The clock speed is wrong in both.</term>
                <c>PSC = 48000 - 1</c> assumes a 48 MHz clock and
                <c>PSC = 16000 - 1</c> assumes 16 MHz; ours runs at 12 MHz, so
                the first counts one tick every 4 ms and the second every
                1.33 ms.  The <c>ARR = 1000 - 1</c> that was meant to make one
                second makes four seconds and one and a third.  Nothing warns
                you; the program simply runs slow, and you have to know the
                clock to know that it is wrong.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Both check the button only when the timer fires.</term>
                The button test is inside <c>if (timerElapsed)</c>, so a press is
                acted on at the next tick rather than when it happens, and two
                presses inside one second become one.  That is the thing the
                interrupt was for.  Compare with two independent <c>if</c>s: a
                press and a tick are unrelated events and each should be handled
                when it arrives.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Both clear the timer flag with a compound assignment.</term>
                <c>TIM14->SR &amp;= ~TIM_SR_UIF;</c> reads the register, clears
                one bit in the copy, and writes the whole thing back — the
                pattern the timers chapter spent a section on
                (<xref ref="subsec-day8-rcw0"/>).  Here it happens to be
                harmless, because <c>TIM14_SR</c> is <c>rc_w0</c> and a 1 written
                to a flag has no effect; the reason to write
                <c>TIM14->SR = ~TIM_SR_UIF;</c> anyway is that the habit is what
                protects you on the registers where it is not harmless.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>ChatGPT's timer never zeroes the count.</term>  Our own
                <c>tim14_ms_interrupt_init()</c> writes <c>TIM14->CNT = 0;</c>
                before enabling the timer, so the first interval is a full one.
                Without it the first interval is however far the counter had
                already got.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Gemini's program does not use the timer interrupt at
                all.</term>  Look at its <c>tim14_init()</c>: no
                <c>TIM_DIER_UIE</c>, no <c>NVIC_EnableIRQ()</c>, and no ISR
                anywhere in the file.  The main loop polls <c>TIM14->SR</c>
                instead, which is the <em>polled</em> version we started from.  It works, and
                it is not what was asked for, and reading the code is the only
                way to notice — the output looks identical.
            </p>
            <p>
                And one that is a matter of judgment rather than correctness:
                neither program's <c>main()</c> ended the way this book's do —
                ChatGPT's had no <c>return</c> at all and Gemini's ended
                <c>return 0;</c>.  Both listings above have been normalized to
                <c>return 1;</c>, and that is the one edit made to them.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-ai-chatgpt" room="compressed">
    <p><term>ChatGPT's answer to Tuesday's assignment.</term> It compiles.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
  while (1) {
    if (timerElapsed) {
      timerElapsed = 0;
      printf("%x\t%d\r\n", counter, counter);
      if (buttonPushed)                 { counter = 0; buttonPushed = 0; }
      else if (counter == MAXCOUNT - 1) { counter = 0; }
      else                              { counter++; }
    }
  }
void TIM14_IRQHandler(void) {
  if (TIM14->SR & TIM_SR_UIF) { TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF; timerElapsed = 1; }
}
void tim14_init(void) {
  RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;  TIM14->PSC = 48000 - 1;
  TIM14->ARR = 1000 - 1;                TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;
  TIM14->DIER |= TIM_DIER_UIE;          NVIC_EnableIRQ(TIM14_IRQn);
  TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;   }
]]></code></program>
    <note>Abridged for the slide: the includes, the prototypes, the volatile declarations and main()'s opening are dropped, and pb4_exti_init() and the EXTI handler are marked "unchanged from our code" in the original. The book has the full listing.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-ai-gemini" room="compressed">
    <p><term>And Gemini's.</term> Also compiles.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
  while(1) {
    if (TIM14->SR & TIM_SR_UIF) {
      TIM14->SR &= ~TIM_SR_UIF;
      printf("%x\t%d\r\n", counter, counter);
      if (buttonPushed) { counter = 0; buttonPushed = 0; }
      else if (counter >= MAXCOUNT - 1) { counter = 0; }
      else { counter++; }
    }
  }
void tim14_init(void) {
  RCC->APBENR2 |= RCC_APBENR2_TIM14EN;  TIM14->PSC = 16000 - 1;
  TIM14->ARR = 1000 - 1;                TIM14->EGR |= TIM_EGR_UG;
  TIM14->CR1 |= TIM_CR1_CEN;   }
]]></code></program>
    <caption>Pick one of the two. Write your answers before anybody talks.</caption>
    <note>Same abridgement as the previous slide. A working reference copy is on Canvas and goes to the whole room before groups form, so that a student whose homework did not run has something to compare against and never has to say so out loud.
    Equipment today: the display still wired from yesterday and verified in Part 2, and the AD2 — needed in Part 9, and the debugging instrument if a write does not land earlier.
    110 minutes, and it sums to 111. If it runs long, trim Part 9 first (it is the stretch), then compress Part 1 from 9 to 6 by cutting the group comparison and keeping the individual written answer. NOT cuttable: Part 8c, and Part 8a's Adafruit beat.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-ai-findings">
    <p><term>What is in there.</term> Your list may have more; these are the ones that change what the board does.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><term>The clock speed is wrong in both.</term> <c>PSC = 48000-1</c> assumes a 48 MHz clock and <c>16000-1</c> assumes 16 MHz; ours is 12 MHz. The <q>one second</q> comes out at four seconds and one and a third. Nothing warns you &#8212; you have to know the clock.</li>
        <li><term>Both check the button only when the timer fires.</term> The test is inside <c>if (timerElapsed)</c>, so a press waits for the next tick and two presses in one second become one. That is what the interrupt was for. Two independent <c>if</c>s: a press and a tick are unrelated events.</li>
        <li><term>Both clear the flag with <c>&amp;=</c>.</term> Harmless on <c>TIM14_SR</c>, which is <c>rc_w0</c> &#8212; but the habit is what protects you on the registers where it is not.</li>
        <li><term>ChatGPT never zeroes <c>TIM14-&gt;CNT</c>,</term> so the first interval is however far the counter had already got. Our own <c>tim14_ms_interrupt_init()</c> does.</li>
        <li><term>Gemini's program does not use the timer interrupt at all.</term> No <c>UIE</c>, no <c>NVIC_EnableIRQ()</c>, no ISR &#8212; the loop polls <c>TIM14-&gt;SR</c>. It works, it is not what was asked for, and the output looks identical.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-verify">
            <title>Part 2: Does the Bus Still Work?</title>
            <p>
                Before anything else: flash <c>helloDisplay.c</c>, unchanged from
                yesterday, and confirm the display lights.  The kits stayed in
                the room overnight, but four wires is four chances for one to be
                out.  Finding that now costs three minutes; finding it at minute
                90, in the middle of debugging a driver you have just written,
                costs what is left of a long session.
            </p>
            <p>
                Four characters on the display is a strong test today, because
                the program that puts them there exercises the whole path: the
                two pins, the bus, the three setup commands and a ten-byte
                write.  Anything wrong anywhere in that and the display is
                blank.
            </p>
            <p>
                A blank display is a wiring problem and not a code problem — the
                code is a file that worked yesterday.  Re-seat all four wires
                against <xref ref="fig-display-wiring"/>, check <c>+</c> is on
                3.3 V and not 5 V, and check that <c>D</c> and <c>C</c> have not
                been swapped.  If it is still blank, flag it now rather than at
                minute 90 — today's crucial step needs a display that talks.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-verify">
    <p><term>Before anything else: flash yesterday's helloDisplay.c, unchanged.</term> The kits stayed in the room overnight, but four wires is four chances for one to be out.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Finding it now costs three minutes. Finding it at minute 90, in the middle of debugging a driver you have just written, costs the rest of a long session.</li>
        <li>Four characters is a <term>strong test</term>: that program exercises the two pins, the bus, the three setup commands and a ten-byte write. Anything wrong anywhere in that and the display is blank.</li>
        <li>A blank display is a <term>wiring</term> problem, not a code problem &#8212; the code is a file that worked yesterday. Re-seat all four, check <c>+</c> is on 3.3 V, check <c>D</c> and <c>C</c> are not swapped.</li>
        <li>Still blank? <term>Flag it now rather than at minute 90.</term> Today's crucial step needs a display that talks.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Three minutes, and it is the cost of having moved the display work to Day 9x. It also gives anyone whose display never lit yesterday a second try with the whole session still ahead.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-display-hardware">
            <title>Part 3: How the Display Is Actually Built</title>
            <p>
                From the reading: 34 LEDs, a grid, and a sweep fast enough to
                fool the eye.  You already know the segment names and the bit
                order they map to (<xref ref="fig-segment-map"/>), because you
                wrote a digit's byte with them yesterday.  What is left is the
                wiring behind them, and the answer to a question you have not
                been given a reason to ask yet: <em>why does a display need its
                own chip?</em>
            </p>
            <p>
                The grid is drawn in <xref ref="fig-four-digit-wiring"/>, which
                is the display's own datasheet.  It is a
                <term>common cathode</term> arrangement: within one digit, all
                eight LEDs have their cathodes tied together and brought out on a
                single pin — the ones marked <c>(COM)</c> — while across digits,
                all four <c>a</c> anodes are tied together, all four <c>b</c>
                anodes are tied together, and so on.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-four-digit-wiring">
                <caption>The internal wiring of the four-digit display, from its
                datasheet.  Every segment is an LED.  Reading down: the eight
                LEDs of each digit share one cathode, brought out on the pin
                marked (COM) above that digit — pins 14, 11, 10 and 6, with pin 7
                serving the colon's two LEDs.  Reading across: all four
                <c>a</c> anodes are connected together and brought out on one
                pin, and likewise for <c>b</c> through <c>g</c> and <c>DP</c> —
                the eight pins along the bottom, 13, 9, 4, 2, 1, 12, 5 and 3 —
                with pin 8 for the colon.  Five cathode pins and nine anode pins
                is 14 wires for 34 LEDs.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/four_digit_wiring.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Work out what that costs and what it forbids.  To light the
                <c>a</c> segment of digit 2, drive the shared <c>a</c> anode high
                and pull digit 2's common cathode low: current has a path through
                exactly one LED, because that is the only place where an
                energized anode meets an energized cathode.  To light
                <c>a</c> of digit 2 <em>and</em> <c>b</c> of digit 3 at the same
                time, you would have to energize both anodes and both cathodes —
                which also lights <c>b</c> of digit 2 and <c>a</c> of digit 3.
                A grid cannot do arbitrary patterns at one instant.  It can do
                one digit at a time, and that is what the sweep is for.
            </p>
            <p>
                The count comes out at four digit cathodes plus one for the
                colon, and eight segment anodes plus one for the colon: 14 pins,
                34 LEDs.  Everything the backpack does — sweeping the four
                digits, holding each one on for its share of the time, keeping
                the whole thing going while your program does something else —
                is that grid, driven continuously.
            </p>
            <p>
                That is an argument, and you can watch it.  The display in front
                of you looks steady, and it is not: at every instant most of it
                is dark.  Two ways to catch it, and they take a minute between
                them.  <term>Wave it.</term>  Pick the breadboard up and move the
                lit display briskly from side to side, and watch what the trail
                does — a light that is genuinely on all the time smears into a
                continuous band, and one that is being switched leaves separate
                images with gaps between them.  <term>Or film it.</term>  Put
                your phone's camera into its slow-motion mode and point it at the
                display, then play the clip back and step through it.  A camera
                exposing for a short enough slice of time catches the display
                part-way through a sweep rather than averaged over one.
            </p>
            <!--
            OPEN, for Petra: the persistence-of-vision beat.

            Three things here are unverified, and the paragraph above is
            deliberately written so that it asserts none of them.

            1. The HT16K33's scan rate is not in the repo, and neither is its
               datasheet, so the text does not say what shutter speed or frame
               rate is needed, and does not claim which of the two routes
               actually works.  One trial with a real display and a real phone
               settles it, and the wording can then get more specific.
            2. The third route, the AD2 on one of the display's segment lines,
               is not written up at all.  Whether any of the fourteen
               display-to-backpack lines is reachable once the backpack is
               seated in the breadboard is not something the repo establishes.
            3. FIGURE NEEDED, and it does not exist: one frame of a swept or
               slow-motion capture with a single digit lit and the rest dark.
               There is nothing in assets/images to build it from.
            -->
            <p>
                Either way, what you are seeing is the sweep.  And it is the
                answer to a question nobody has asked out loud yet: <em>why does
                a display need its own chip?</em>  Because the image only exists
                while something is redrawing it, thousands of times a second,
                and it never gets to stop.  If that something were your program,
                every other thing you wanted the STM32C031C6 to do would have to
                be fitted around a refresh loop with a deadline that never ends.
                The backpack is what takes that job.
            </p>
            <p>
                <xref ref="fig-ht16k33-block"/> is the HT16K33 from the inside.
                The I2C interface is on the left; the display RAM in the middle
                holds which LEDs are on; the row and common drivers on the right
                are the fourteen pins going to the display; and the internal RC
                oscillator with the timing generator is what runs the sweep.
                That oscillator is off when the chip powers up, which is the
                first thing our driver will have to fix.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ht16k33-block">
                <caption>The HT16K33's block diagram, from its datasheet.  SDA
                and SCL enter the I2C controller on the left, which is the only
                way into the chip.  The display RAM — 16 bytes of it — holds
                which LEDs are lit, and the common-scan and row-driver outputs on
                the right are the pins that go to the display's grid.  The
                internal RC oscillator and timing generator sweep that grid from
                the RAM, continuously and without any further instruction.  The
                key-scan and key-data blocks belong to the chip's other job,
                reading a keypad matrix, which we do not use.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/ht16k33_block.svg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-segment-map" ref="fig-segment-map">
    <caption>Yesterday's map, for reference: the segment names are the order the bits are in. Today is the wiring behind them.</caption>
    <note>Ten seconds, as a reminder — they wrote a digit's byte with this yesterday. Do not re-teach it. The colon's two LEDs are D5 and D6 here.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-grid" ref="fig-four-digit-wiring" stack="yes" room="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><term>Common cathode.</term> Within one digit, all eight LEDs share a cathode, brought out on the pin marked (COM). Across digits, all four <c>a</c> anodes are tied together, all four <c>b</c> anodes, and so on.</li>
        <li>So: <term>how do you turn on the <c>a</c> segment of digit 2?</term></li>
        <li>And then: <term>how do you turn on <c>a</c> of digit 2 <em>and</em> <c>b</c> of digit 3, at the same instant?</term></li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Five cathode pins and nine anode pins — 14 wires for 34 LEDs. Now find the path to one LED.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-grid-answer">
    <p><term>One digit at a time, and that is what the sweep is for.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><c>a</c> of digit 2: drive the shared <c>a</c> anode high and pull digit 2's common cathode low. Current has a path through exactly one LED, because that is the only place an energized anode meets an energized cathode.</li>
        <li><c>a</c> of digit 2 <em>and</em> <c>b</c> of digit 3: you would energize both anodes and both cathodes &#8212; which also lights <c>b</c> of digit 2 and <c>a</c> of digit 3. <term>A grid cannot do arbitrary patterns at one instant.</term></li>
        <li>It can do one digit at a time. Sweep the four fast enough and your eye sees all four at once, which is what the backpack does, continuously, forever.</li>
        <li>Count: four digit cathodes plus one for the colon, eight segment anodes plus one for the colon &#8212; <term>14 pins, 34 LEDs</term>.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>


            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-pov">
    <p><term>Your display looks steady. It is not.</term> At every instant most of it is dark &#8212; and you can catch it, two ways, in about a minute.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><term>Wave it.</term> Pick the breadboard up and move the lit display briskly side to side. A light that is genuinely on all the time smears into a <term>continuous band</term>; one that is being switched leaves <term>separate images with gaps</term> between them.</li>
        <li><term>Or film it.</term> Phone camera, slow-motion mode, then step through the clip. A short enough exposure catches the display part-way through a sweep instead of averaged over one.</li>
        <li>Which is the answer to a question nobody has asked out loud yet: <term>why does a display need its own chip?</term> Because the image only exists while something is redrawing it, thousands of times a second, and it never gets to stop.</li>
        <li>If that something were <term>your program</term>, everything else you wanted the STM32C031C6 to do would have to fit around a refresh loop whose deadline never ends. The backpack is what takes that job.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>OPEN — see the note in the book at this point. The HT16K33's scan rate is not in the repo, so neither route is claimed to work at a stated shutter speed; try one before class. A figure for this beat does not exist.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-block" ref="fig-ht16k33-block">
    <caption>The HT16K33 from the inside: I2C in on the left, display RAM in the middle, the 14 grid pins on the right — and an internal oscillator that is off at power-up.</caption>
    <note>That last one is the point: the oscillator running the sweep starts off, so the first thing the driver does is turn it on. The key-scan blocks are the chip's other job, reading a keypad matrix, which we do not use.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-commands">
            <title>Part 4: Reading the Command Table</title>
            <p>
                Most I2C devices have registers inside them, and reach them
                through commands the datasheet defines.  The HT16K33's commands
                are one byte each, sent as the data of an ordinary write —
                exactly what the three given lines at the top of
                <c>helloDisplay.c</c> were doing, except that this
                time the byte will mean something.
            </p>
            <p>
                The command table is on pages 24–25 of the HT16K33 datasheet.
                Its layout takes a moment to read: the eight columns
                <c>D15</c> down to <c>D8</c> are the bits of the command byte,
                most significant first; a column with a fixed 0 or 1 is part of
                the command's identity, and a column with a letter in it is an
                option you fill in.  Three rows are all we need
                (<xref ref="fig-ht16k33-cmd-table"/>).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ht16k33-cmd-table">
                <caption>Rows of the HT16K33 command table, pages 24–25 of its
                datasheet.  The eight middle columns are the bits of the command
                byte, D15 down to D8; a 0 or a 1 there is part of the command's
                identity and a letter is an option you supply.  <em>System
                setup</em> is 0 0 1 0 X X X S, where S is the internal
                oscillator: 0 leaves it off, 1 turns it on.  <em>Display
                setup</em> is 1 0 0 0 X B1 B0 D, where D turns the display on and
                B1 B0 choose the blink rate — 00 for steady, then 2 Hz, 1 Hz and
                0.5 Hz.  <em>Dimming set</em> is 1 1 1 0 P3 P2 P1 P0, the
                <term>duty cycle</term> — the fraction of each refresh cycle the
                LEDs are actually lit — in sixteenths, where 0000 is 1/16 and
                1111 is 16/16.  The <em>Def.</em> column on the right is each
                command with its options at their default.  Two more rows are
                here: <em>display data address pointer</em>, at the top, is what
                <xref ref="subsec-day10-ram"/> uses to say where a block of
                display data starts; and <em>ROW/INT set</em> chooses whether the
                shared pins drive the display's rows or read a keypad, and its
                default — A0H, ROW driver output — is the one we want, so we
                never send it.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/ht16k33_cmd_pointer_system.png" width="94%"/>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/ht16k33_cmd_display.png" width="94%"/>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/ht16k33_cmd_dimming.png" width="94%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-ht16k33-commands">
                <title>Build the Three Startup Bytes</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Work these out as binary and then write them as hex.  All
                        three are in <xref ref="fig-ht16k33-cmd-table"/>.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-cmd-t1">
                    <statement><p>System setup is <c>0b0010000S</c>.  What byte
                    turns the oscillator on?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-cmd-t2">
                    <statement><p>Display setup is <c>0b1000XB1B0D</c>.  What
                    byte turns the display on and not blinking?  What byte would
                    turn it on and blinking at 1 Hz?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-cmd-t3">
                    <statement><p>Dimming set is <c>0b1110P3P2P1P0</c>.  What
                    byte gives full brightness?  Half?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-cmd-t4">
                    <statement><p>All three are one-byte writes to the device.
                    Which library function sends one, and what are its two
                    arguments?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                The answers are <c>0x21</c>, <c>0x81</c> and <c>0xEF</c>:
                <c>0b00100001</c> is system setup with the oscillator bit set,
                <c>0b10000001</c> is display setup with D set and both blink bits
                clear, and <c>0b11101111</c> is dimming set with all four duty
                bits set.  Blinking at 1 Hz is <c>0b10000101</c>, <c>0x85</c>,
                because 1 Hz is B1 B0 = 1 0.  Half brightness needs the
                encoding read carefully, because it counts from one rather than
                zero — 0000 is 1/16 and 1111 is 16/16 — so eight sixteenths is
                P3–P0 = 0111 and the byte is <c>0xE7</c>.  And each of them goes out with
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, ...)</c> — one function call per
                command, one transaction on the wire per call
                (<xref ref="fig-ht16k33-addr-frame"/>).
            </p>
            <p>
                Rather than writing those three numbers into your code, the
                header you are given names their pieces:
                <c>HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD</c> and <c>HT16K33_OSC_ON</c> for the
                first, <c>HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD</c>, <c>HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON</c>,
                <c>HT16K33_BLINK_OFF</c> and <c>HT16K33_BLINK_1HZ</c> for the
                second, <c>HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD</c> for the third.  Each name is
                one field of the table, so a line of driver code reads the way
                the datasheet row does: the command, ORed with its options.  That
                is the structure to copy the next time you write one of these —
                the datasheet's own layout becomes the header file's layout.
            </p>
            <p>
                Those three bytes are the first function of your driver.  It has
                to run before the display will show anything at all, and it is
                three calls long.  You write it in <xref ref="subsec-day10-first-digit"/>.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-cmd-table" ref="fig-ht16k33-cmd-table" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Most I2C devices have registers inside them, reached through commands the datasheet defines. The HT16K33's commands are <term>one byte each</term>, sent as the data of an ordinary write &#8212; exactly what the three given lines at the top of
                <c>helloDisplay.c</c> were doing, except that this time the byte means something.</li>
        <li>Reading the table: the eight middle columns are the bits of that byte, D15 down to D8. A <term>0 or 1 is part of the command's identity</term>; a <term>letter is an option you fill in</term>.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Datasheet pp. 24–25. Three of these rows are all today needs.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-cmd-derive" room="yes">
    <p><term>Build the three startup bytes.</term> Binary first, then hex.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
   system setup     0b0010 0 0 0 S        S = oscillator on/off
   display setup    0b1000 X B1 B0 D      D = display on;  B1 B0 = blink rate
   dimming set      0b1110 P3 P2 P1 P0    duty cycle in sixteenths:
                                          0000 is 1/16, 1111 is 16/16
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>What byte turns the <term>oscillator on</term>?</li>
        <li>What byte turns the display <term>on and not blinking</term>? And on, blinking at 1 Hz?</li>
        <li>What byte gives <term>full brightness</term>? Half?</li>
        <li>All three are one-byte writes to the device. Which library function sends one, and what are its two arguments?</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-cmd-answer">
    <p><term>0x21, 0x81, 0xEF</term> &#8212; and you write the names, not the numbers.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
0b0010 0001 = 0x21   system setup,  oscillator bit set
0b1000 0001 = 0x81   display setup, D set, both blink bits clear
0b1110 1111 = 0xEF   dimming set,   all four duty bits set

0b1000 0101 = 0x85   ... on, blinking at 1 Hz  (B1 B0 = 1 0)
0b1110 0111 = 0xE7   ... half: 0111 -> 7 -> (7+1)/16 = 8/16

i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD | HT16K33_OSC_ON);
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The header names every field of the table: <c>HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD</c>, <c>HT16K33_OSC_ON</c>, <c>HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD</c>, <c>HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON</c>, <c>HT16K33_BLINK_OFF</c>, <c>HT16K33_BLINK_1HZ</c>, <c>HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD</c>.</li>
        <li>So a line of driver code reads the way the datasheet row does: <term>the command, ORed with its options</term>. That is the structure to copy the next time you write one of these &#8212; the datasheet's layout becomes the header file's layout.</li>
        <li>Those three bytes are the first function of your driver, and you write it in Part 8b.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-ram">
            <title>Part 5: The Display RAM, and the Byte That Is Always Zero</title>
            <p>
                Commands configure the chip.  Getting a pattern onto the display
                means writing its <term>display RAM</term>, and the layout of
                that RAM is the one thing on the datasheet worth reading slowly
                (<xref ref="fig-display-ram-map"/>).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-display-ram-map">
                <caption>The HT16K33's display RAM, from its datasheet: sixteen
                bytes, two per common line.  The left column names the common
                line — the chip drives eight of them, COM0 to COM7, of which our
                display uses five — and the two addresses beside each hold that
                line's sixteen possible LEDs, ROW0 to ROW7 in
                the first byte and ROW8 to ROW15 in the second.  The lower table
                gives the bit order within a byte, D7 down to D0 mapping to
                ROW7 down to ROW0.  A seven-segment digit uses only the first
                eight rows, so for us the second byte of every pair is always 0 —
                and the even addresses 0x00, 0x02, 0x04, 0x06 and 0x08 are the
                five that carry anything.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/display_ram_map.svg" width="55%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Two bytes per digit, then, and the second one is always zero for
                us — the HT16K33 can drive sixteen LEDs per common line and our
                digits use eight.  The first byte is the segments, in the order
                the reading's names suggest: bit 0 is <c>a</c>, bit 1 is
                <c>b</c>, on up to bit 6 for <c>g</c>, and bit 7 is the decimal
                point.  Written out as a binary literal that is
                <c>0b</c> DP <c>g f e d c b a</c>, most significant bit first, so
                that a <c>0</c> — every segment but <c>g</c> — is
                <c>0b00111111</c>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Five pairs, at addresses 0, 2, 4, 6 and 8: digit 1, digit 2, the
                colon, digit 3, digit 4.  The colon sits in the middle because
                that is where it sits on the display.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-make-pattern">
                <title>Make a Pattern</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        On paper, before any of this is code.  The buffer is ten
                        bytes, <c>display_buffer[0]</c> through
                        <c>display_buffer[9]</c>.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-pattern-t1">
                    <statement><p>Fill in all ten bytes to light every segment
                    and every decimal point of all four digits, as drawn in
                    <xref ref="fig-segment-map"/>.  Write each byte in
                    binary.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-pattern-t2">
                    <statement><p>Now fill in all ten to display
                    <c>0 1 2 3</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-pattern-t3">
                    <statement><p>Which byte would you change to add a decimal
                    point after the second digit, and what would you change it
                    to?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Every segment of every digit is <c>0b11111111</c> in bytes 0, 2,
                6 and 8; every other byte, including the colon's pair at 4 and 5,
                is 0.  <c>0 1 2 3</c> is
                <c>0b00111111</c>, <c>0b00000110</c>, <c>0b01011011</c>,
                <c>0b01001111</c> in the same four places — worth deriving once
                by hand, after which you will use the <c>numbertable[]</c> array
                in the header, which holds the sixteen patterns for 0 through F.  A decimal
                point after the second digit means setting bit 7 of
                <c>display_buffer[2]</c>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Two questions the paper exercise raises.  <em>What lights the
                colon?</em>  It is the pair at address 4, and which bit within it
                is not on the datasheet pages we have — that one is worth
                finding by experiment, and the clock in tonight's homework needs
                it.  And <em>why send the zeros at all,</em> if the second byte
                of every pair does nothing?
            </p>
            <p>
                That second one has a real answer, and without it <q>always
                write 0</q> is just a rule to memorize.  The HT16K33 has one
                pointer into its display RAM.  You set it once, at the start of
                the transaction, and it <term>advances by one every time a byte
                arrives</term> — that is what the datasheet means by the display
                data address pointer, and it is why writing ten bytes from
                address 0 fills addresses 0 through 9.  The zeros are not
                decoration; they are what moves the pointer past the odd
                addresses so that the next real byte lands on the next digit.
                Leave them out and the second digit's pattern goes to address 1,
                which is the top half of digit 1.
            </p>
            <p>
                All ten bytes go in <em>one</em> transaction
                (<xref ref="fig-ht16k33-page-write"/>), and they have to: a STOP
                ends the transaction, and after a STOP the next write starts
                over with a new address.  The library call for
                <q>address the device, say where inside it, and send this many
                bytes</q> is <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> — the multi-byte function
                you have already called without knowing what it did, and this
                is what it was for.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-ht16k33-page-write">
                <caption>Writing the whole display in one transaction — the
                HT16K33 datasheet calls this a page write, on page 22.  After the
                START and the address comes one command byte, and because the
                display data address pointer command is 0 0 0 0 A3 A2 A1 A0, a
                command byte of 0x00 means <q>start at display RAM address 0</q>.
                Every data byte after it is stored where the pointer is and moves
                the pointer on by one, so ten data bytes fill addresses 0 through
                9.  Each byte is acknowledged, and one STOP ends the whole
                thing.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/ht16k33_page_write.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-ram" ref="fig-display-ram-map" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Sixteen bytes, <term>two per common line</term>: ROW0&#8211;ROW7 in the first and ROW8&#8211;ROW15 in the second. A seven-segment digit uses only the first eight rows, so <term>the second byte of every pair is always 0 for us</term>.</li>
        <li>That leaves five even addresses carrying anything: <c>0x00</c>, <c>0x02</c>, <c>0x04</c>, <c>0x06</c>, <c>0x08</c> &#8212; digit 1, digit 2, <term>the colon</term>, digit 3, digit 4. The colon sits in the middle because that is where it sits on the display.</li>
        <li>Within a digit's byte: bit 0 is <c>a</c>, up to bit 6 for <c>g</c>, and bit 7 is the decimal point. Written as a binary literal that reads <c>0b</c> DP g f e d c b a, so a <c>0</c> &#8212; every segment but <c>g</c> &#8212; is <c>0b00111111</c>.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Two bytes per position, and we write the odd one as 0 every time. The next slide says why it has to be written at all.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-pattern" ref="fig-segment-map" room="yes">
    <p><term>On paper, before any of this is code.</term> The buffer is ten bytes, <c>display_buffer[0]</c> through <c>display_buffer[9]</c>.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>Fill in all ten to light <term>every segment and every decimal point of all four digits</term>. Each byte in binary.</li>
        <li>Now fill in all ten to display <c>0 1 2 3</c>.</li>
        <li>Which byte would you change to add a decimal point after the second digit, and what to?</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-pattern-answer">
    <p><term>Everything on, then 0 1 2 3:</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
all segments:   [0] 0b11111111   [2] 0b11111111   [6] 0b11111111   [8] 0b11111111
                [1] [3] [4] [5] [7] [9]  all 0

  "0 1 2 3":    [0] 0b00111111   a b c d e f
                [2] 0b00000110   b c
                [6] 0b01011011   a b d e g
                [8] 0b01001111   a b c d g

decimal point after digit 2:  set bit 7 of display_buffer[2]
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Worth deriving once by hand, after which you use <c>numbertable[]</c> from the header, which holds the sixteen patterns for 0 through F.</li>
        <li>The colon is the pair at address 4, and <term>which bit inside it lights the colon is not on the datasheet pages we have</term>. Find it by experiment &#8212; tonight's clock needs it.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-pointer" ref="fig-ht16k33-page-write" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li><term>Why send the zeros at all,</term> if the second byte of each pair does nothing? Because the HT16K33 has <term>one pointer</term> into its display RAM. You set it once, in the command byte at the start of the transaction, and it <term>advances by one every time a byte arrives</term>.</li>
        <li>So ten bytes from address 0 fill addresses 0 through 9. Leave the zeros out and the second digit's pattern lands at address 1, which is the top half of digit 1.</li>
        <li>And all ten go in <term>one</term> transaction: a STOP ends the transaction, and after a STOP the next write starts over at whatever address its own command byte names.</li>
        <li>The library call for <q>address the device, say where inside it, send this many bytes</q> is <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> &#8212; the one you called yesterday without knowing what it did, and this is what it was for.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>One command byte says where to start; every data byte after it moves the pointer on by one.</caption>
</slide>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-pins">
            <title>Part 6a: The Pins, and Why They Must Be Open-Drain</title>
            <p>
                A peripheral inside the chip has to reach the outside world
                through pins, and which pins it can use is not up to us
                (<xref ref="fig-i2c-pins"/>).  I2C1's SCL and SDA signals are
                available on several pairs, and the pair we use is
                <term>PB8 for SCL and PB9 for SDA</term>, because those two are
                the Nucleo's Arduino-standard I2C pins — D15 and D14 on the
                header — and every Arduino-shaped board puts I2C there.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-pins">
                <caption>The Nucleo-C031C6's pinout with every I2C-capable pin
                marked in orange.  I2C1's SCL and SDA appear on several pairs;
                the one this course uses is D15 (PB8, SCL) and D14 (PB9, SDA),
                marked <q>standard Arduino choice</q>, because that is where
                every Arduino-shaped board puts its I2C bus.  The green labels
                are the STM32C031C6 port pin behind each header pin.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_pins_nucleo.svg" width="90%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Getting a peripheral's signal onto a pin is the alternate-function
                mechanism you first used for the UART: put the pin in alternate
                function mode with <c>MODER</c>, then choose <em>which</em>
                alternate function with <c>AFR</c>.  The number to choose is in
                the datasheet, not the reference manual — <em>DS13867 Table 15,
                Port B alternate function mapping</em>, where the PB8 row and the
                PB9 row both name I2C1 under <term>AF6</term>.
            </p>
            <p>
                Then one thing that is new.  Both pins have to be configured
                <term>open-drain</term>, through the GPIO output type register
                <c>GPIOx_OTYPER</c> (RM0490 §6.4.2), where a 1 in bit
                <m>y</m> means <q>pin <m>y</m> is open-drain</q> and 0 — the
                reset value — means push-pull.  A push-pull output has two
                transistors, one pulling the pin up to 3.3 V and one pulling it
                down, and it always drives the pin to one rail or the other.  An
                open-drain output has only the pull-down: writing a 0 pulls the
                pin LOW, and writing a 1 turns the transistor off and leaves the
                pin floating, so the external pull-up sets the level.
            </p>
            <p>
                On this bus that is not a preference.  SDA is driven by the
                controller for eight bits and by the device for the ninth,
                every single byte, and the two ends do not always agree about
                what it should be.  <xref ref="fig-open-drain"/> is what that
                moment looks like in the two arrangements.  If both ends were
                push-pull, one driving HIGH and one driving LOW puts a transistor
                from 3.3 V and a transistor to ground both switched on across the
                same wire — a short circuit, limited only by what the two output
                stages can survive.  With open-drain outputs there is no such
                path: the strongest thing any device can do is pull the line LOW,
                the only route from 3.3 V is through the pull-up resistor, and
                the worst case of two devices disagreeing is that the line reads
                LOW.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-open-drain">
                <caption>The same disagreement, wired two ways.  On the left, two
                push-pull outputs on one wire: each has a transistor to 3.3 V and
                one to ground, and when one drives HIGH while the other drives
                LOW both are switched on across the same wire and current runs
                straight from the supply to ground.  Nothing decides what the
                wire's voltage is.  On the right, the same two devices as
                open-drain outputs: the transistor to 3.3 V is gone and a single
                pull-up resistor takes its place, so one device pulling LOW while
                the other releases has no path except through that resistor.  The
                wire reads LOW, no damage is possible, and — because releasing is
                the only way to send a 1 — a device that releases and then reads
                LOW knows somebody else is pulling.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/open_drain_vs_push_pull.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Which is also why the pull-up matters.  The ACK works because of this, and so
                do two features this course does not use but the protocol
                depends on: several controllers sharing one bus, and a device
                holding SCL LOW to ask for more time.
                <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-opendrain"/> has those two.
            </p>
            <p>
                The pull-ups themselves are typically 5 kΩ to 10 kΩ, and on this
                bus we do not fit any: both devices this course uses come on
                breakout boards that carry their own.  That is worth checking on
                the datasheet of anything you add.  With no pull-up anywhere the
                line can never be released HIGH — nothing on an open-drain bus
                drives HIGH, as the paragraph above says, so the level is simply
                undefined.  The reason the value is a range rather than a number
                is that it is squeezed from both ends: too small and the device
                pulling LOW has to sink more current than it can while still
                holding the line below the receiver's threshold; too large and
                the resistor charges the bus's own capacitance too slowly for the
                line to get back up before the next clock edge.
                <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-opendrain"/> does that arithmetic.
            </p>
            <p>
                One line of Arduino did all of that.  <c>Wire.begin()</c> put
                both pins in alternate-function mode, selected the right
                alternate function, made them open-drain, enabled the
                peripheral's clock and set the bus speed.  It could, because the
                board's designers knew which pins the library was allowed to
                assume.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-pins" ref="fig-i2c-pins" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>I2C1's SCL and SDA come out on several pin pairs; ours is <term>PB8 for SCL and PB9 for SDA</term>, boxed here &#8212; D15 and D14 on the header, where every Arduino-shaped board puts its I2C bus.</li>
        <li>Getting the peripheral onto the pins is the alternate-function mechanism from the UART: alternate-function mode in <c>MODER</c>, then <em>which</em> one in <c>AFR</c>.</li>
        <li>The number is in the <term>datasheet</term>, not the reference manual: DS13867 Table 15, <em>Port B alternate function mapping</em>. The PB8 and PB9 rows both name I2C1 under <term>AF6</term>.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Find PB8 and PB9 in the boxed pair, then find their AF6 column in Table 15.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-opendrain" room="yes">
    <p><term>And one thing that is new: both pins have to be open-drain.</term> <c>GPIOx_OTYPER</c>, RM0490 &#167;6.4.2 &#8212; 1 is open-drain, 0 (the reset value) is push-pull.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
GPIOB->OTYPER |= (GPIO_OTYPER_OT8 | GPIO_OTYPER_OT9);

push-pull:    writing 1 drives the pin to 3.3 V, 0 drives it to 0 V
open-drain:   writing 0 pulls the pin LOW; writing 1 lets go, and the
              external pull-up sets the level
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>On this bus that is not a preference: <term>SDA changes hands every ninth bit</term>, and the two ends do not always agree about what it should be.</li>
        <li><term>Before we look: if both ends were push-pull for that one moment, one driving HIGH and one driving LOW &#8212; what is across that wire?</term></li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-opendrain-fig" ref="fig-open-drain">
    <caption>A short circuit. Left: both ends push-pull — 3.3 V straight to ground through two transistors, and nothing decides the level. Right: the same disagreement open-drain — the only path from the supply is the pull-up, and the wire reads LOW.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-opendrain-more">
    <p><term>What else that buys, and the one number it leaves you.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>The strongest thing any device can do is pull the line LOW, so two devices disagreeing is never worse than a LOW &#8212; and a device that releases and then reads LOW knows somebody else is pulling. Two more features rest on exactly that, and the Reference section has both: <term>several controllers</term> sharing one bus and sorting out who wins, and a device <term>holding SCL LOW</term> to ask for more time.</li>
        <li>Pull-ups are typically 5 k&#8486; to 10 k&#8486;, and we fit none &#8212; both devices this course uses carry their own. The value is squeezed from both ends: <term>too small</term> and the device pulling LOW cannot sink enough current to hold the line below threshold; <term>too large</term> and the resistor charges the bus capacitance too slowly to get back up before the next clock edge.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-wire-begin">
    <p><term>One line of Arduino did all of that.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
Wire.begin();
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>It put both pins in alternate-function mode, selected the right alternate function, made them open-drain, enabled the peripheral's clock and set the bus speed.</li>
        <li>It could, because the board's designers knew which pins the library was allowed to assume. On a board nobody has written a <c>Wire</c> for, somebody has to do what we just did.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-timingr">
            <title>Part 6b: I2C_TIMINGR, and What PRESC Is For</title>
            <p>
                The last thing the peripheral needs is the clock: how long SCL
                spends HIGH, how long it spends LOW, and how long to wait around
                the edges before changing or sampling SDA.  All of it lives in
                one register, <c>I2C_TIMINGR</c>, in five fields — <c>PRESC</c>,
                <c>SCLL</c>, <c>SCLH</c>, <c>SDADEL</c> and <c>SCLDEL</c>.
            </p>
            <p>
                You are not expected to compute those from the I2C
                specification's timing requirements.  The reference manual has
                already done it, for a set of common peripheral clock
                frequencies, in <em>RM0490 §23.4.10, I2C_TIMINGR register
                configuration examples</em> — three tables, one per clock
                frequency, reproduced in
                <xref ref="fig-i2c-timing-tables"/>.  Our peripheral clock is
                12 MHz, which is where the interesting part starts.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-timing-tables">
                <caption>The three worked examples in RM0490 §23.4.10: Table 94
                for a peripheral clock of 8 MHz, Table 95 for 16 MHz and Table 96
                for 48 MHz.  Read down the Standard-mode 100 kHz column of each.
                SCLL is 0x13, SCLH is 0xF, SDADEL is 0x2 and SCLDEL is 0x4 in all
                three; the only field that changes with the clock is PRESC, which
                is 0x1, 0x3 and 0xB.  There is no table for 12 MHz.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_timing_8mhz.png" width="88%"/>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_timing_16mhz.png" width="88%"/>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_timing_48mhz.png" width="88%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-timingr">
                <title>Find Our Row — Except There Isn't One</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Open RM0490 §23.4.10, or read the three tables in
                        <xref ref="fig-i2c-timing-tables"/>.  Our peripheral
                        clock is 12 MHz and we want Standard mode, 100 kHz.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-timingr-t1">
                    <statement><p>Read the 100 kHz column of all three tables.
                    Which of the five fields are the same in every table, and
                    which one is not?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-timingr-t2">
                    <statement><p>PRESC is 0x1 at 8 MHz, 0x3 at 16 MHz and 0xB
                    at 48 MHz.  The prescaler divides the peripheral clock by
                    PRESC + 1.  Work out what each of those three settings
                    divides its clock down to.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-timingr-t3">
                    <statement><p>Now write down the five values for a 12 MHz
                    peripheral clock, and say where each one came
                    from.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-timingr-t4">
                    <statement><p>The 100 kHz column of Table 94 says
                    <c>t_SCLL</c> is 20 × 250 ns and <c>t_SCLH</c> is
                    16 × 250 ns.  Where does 250 ns come from, and why is it the
                    same number in all three tables?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-timingr-t5">
                    <statement><p>Now check it against the bus you already have
                    running.  Put the AD2's cursors on two adjacent SCL rising
                    edges in your capture and read the period off.  Write down
                    what you measured, and whether it matches what you just
                    derived.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Working it through: 8 MHz divided by (0x1 + 1) is 4 MHz;
                16 MHz divided by (0x3 + 1) is 4 MHz; 48 MHz divided by
                (0xB + 1) — that is 12 — is 4 MHz.  Every row of every table
                divides its clock down to the same 4 MHz, and 250 ns is that
                clock's period.  Everything after the prescaler is counted in
                units of 250 ns, which is exactly why the other four fields do
                not change: 20 of them LOW and 16 of them HIGH is 5.0 µs and
                4.0 µs whatever clock the chip started from.  Those two do not
                quite add to the 10 µs a 100 kHz clock needs, and the table says
                why — its own <c>t_SCL</c> row reads <q>~10 µs</q>, footnoted
                <q>t_SCL is greater than t_SCLL + t_SCLH due to SCL internal
                detection delay</q>.  The missing microsecond is the peripheral
                synchronizing to its own clock line, and 10 µs is what you will
                measure.
            </p>
            <p>
                So PRESC is not a magic constant belonging to a board.  It is
                <m>f_\text{I2CCLK} / 4\,\text{MHz} - 1</m>, and for our 12 MHz
                clock it is 2.  There is no 12 MHz table, and we did not need
                one:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
  // Timing settings: Standard mode (100kHz) with 12MHz I2CCLK
  I2C1->TIMINGR &= 0x0; // clear timing register
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0x2  << I2C_TIMINGR_PRESC_Pos);
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0x4  << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLDEL_Pos) | (0x2 << I2C_TIMINGR_SDADEL_Pos);
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0xf << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLH_Pos) | (0x13 << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLL_Pos);
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                If you ever move this code to a board running at a different
                speed, that is the one number to change — and now you know how
                to work it out rather than looking for a table that may not
                exist.  The measurement you can make to check it is the one in
                <xref ref="fig-waveforms-setup"/>: put the cursors on two
                adjacent SCL rising edges and read 10 µs.
            </p>
            <p>
                That is the whole of initialization.  Pins first, then the
                peripheral, and one ordering detail in the middle: the timing
                register may only be written while the peripheral is disabled,
                so <c>PE</c> is cleared before <c>TIMINGR</c> is touched and set
                again afterwards.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void i2c1_init(void){
  // Configure pins PB8 and PB9
  // Enable clock access to GPIOB (for PB8 and PB9)
  RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOBEN;
  // Set PB8 and PB9 to alternate function mode
  GPIOB->MODER = (GPIOB->MODER & ~GPIO_MODER_MODE8_Msk) | (GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE8_Pos);
  GPIOB->MODER = (GPIOB->MODER & ~GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Msk) | (GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE9_Pos);
  // Next, select AF6 for these pins.
  GPIOB->AFR[1] = ((GPIOB->AFR[1]) & ~GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL8_Msk) | (GPIO_AF6 << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL8_Pos);
  GPIOB->AFR[1] = ((GPIOB->AFR[1]) & ~GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Msk) | (GPIO_AF6 << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL9_Pos);
  // Set PB8 and PB9 to open drain
  GPIOB->OTYPER |= (GPIO_OTYPER_OT8 | GPIO_OTYPER_OT9);
  // Enable pull-ups for PB8 and PB9 if needed: our devices have their own pull-ups.

  // Configure the i2c module
  // Enable clock access to I2C1
  RCC->APBENR1 |= RCC_APBENR1_I2C1EN;
  // Enter reset mode (disable peripheral)
  I2C1->CR1 &= ~I2C_CR1_PE;
  // Timing settings: Standard mode (100kHz) with 12MHz I2CCLK
  I2C1->TIMINGR &= 0x0; // clear timing register
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0x2  << I2C_TIMINGR_PRESC_Pos);
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0x4  << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLDEL_Pos) | (0x2 << I2C_TIMINGR_SDADEL_Pos);
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0xf << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLH_Pos) | (0x13 << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLL_Pos);
  // Exit reset mode (enable peripheral)
  I2C1->CR1 |= I2C_CR1_PE;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                <c>AFR[1]</c> rather than <c>AFR[0]</c> for the same reason
                <c>EXTICR[1]</c> was not <c>EXTICR[2]</c> this week: the
                alternate-function selection is two 32-bit registers, one for
                pins 0–7 and one for pins 8–15, and PB8 and PB9 are in the second
                one.  The reference manual calls it <c>GPIOx_AFRH</c>, which is
                why the field names read <c>GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL8</c>.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-timingr-intro">
    <ul>
        <li>The last thing the peripheral needs is the clock: how long SCL is HIGH, how long it is LOW, and how long to wait around the edges before changing or sampling SDA. One register, <c>I2C_TIMINGR</c>, five fields: <c>PRESC</c>, <c>SCLL</c>, <c>SCLH</c>, <c>SDADEL</c>, <c>SCLDEL</c>.</li>
        <li>You are not expected to compute those from the I2C specification's timing requirements. RM0490 &#167;23.4.10 has done it &#8212; three worked examples, one per peripheral clock: 8 MHz, 16 MHz, 48 MHz.</li>
        <li><term>Our peripheral clock is 12 MHz.</term> There is no table for it. Read the Standard-mode 100 kHz column of all three and work out what to write.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-timing-tables" ref="fig-i2c-timing-tables">
    <caption>Tables 94, 95 and 96 — 8, 16 and 48 MHz. Open RM0490 §23.4.10 on your own screen; read the 100 kHz column of each and see what does and does not change.</caption>
    <note>Three tables stacked will not project legibly at this size, so send them to RM0490 §23.4.10 rather than reading them off the wall. The point is only that there are three of them and none says 12 MHz.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-presc" room="yes">
    <p><term>Only one field changes.</term> Read the 100 kHz column of each table:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
                8 MHz     16 MHz    48 MHz     12 MHz (ours)
   PRESC        0x1       0x3       0xB            ?
   SCLL         0x13      0x13      0x13
   SCLH         0xF       0xF       0xF
   SDADEL       0x2       0x2       0x2
   SCLDEL       0x4       0x4       0x4
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The prescaler divides the peripheral clock by <term>PRESC + 1</term>. Work out what each of those three settings divides its clock down to.</li>
        <li>Then: what is PRESC for 12 MHz &#8212; and where did the other four numbers come from, given that they are the same in every table?</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-presc-answer">
    <p><term>Every table divides down to the same 4 MHz.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
   8 MHz / (0x1 + 1) =  8/2  = 4 MHz     -> tick = 250 ns
  16 MHz / (0x3 + 1) = 16/4  = 4 MHz     -> tick = 250 ns
  48 MHz / (0xB + 1) = 48/12 = 4 MHz     -> tick = 250 ns
  12 MHz / (0x2 + 1) = 12/3  = 4 MHz        PRESC = 0x2

  SCLL = 0x13 -> 20 x 250 ns = 5.0 us    SCL LOW
  SCLH = 0xF  -> 16 x 250 ns = 4.0 us    SCL HIGH    9.0 us ... plus ~1 us
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Everything after the prescaler is counted in <term>units of 250 ns</term>, which is why the other four fields never change: 20 ticks LOW and 16 HIGH is the same 9.0 µs whatever clock the chip started from.</li>
        <li>9.0 is not 10 &#8212; and the table says why. Its <c>t_SCL</c> row reads <q>~10 µs</q>, footnoted <q>greater than t_SCLL + t_SCLH due to SCL internal detection delay</q>. That missing microsecond is the peripheral synchronizing to its own clock line, and 10 µs is what you measure.</li>
        <li>So PRESC is not a constant belonging to a board: it is <term>f_I2CCLK / 4 MHz &#8722; 1</term>. Move this code to a faster board and that is the one number to change.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-init-code" room="compressed">
    <p><term>i2c1_init()</term> &#8212; the pins first, then the peripheral:</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
  RCC->IOPENR |= RCC_IOPENR_GPIOBEN;                       // clock to GPIOB
  GPIOB->MODER = (GPIOB->MODER & ~GPIO_MODER_MODE8_Msk)    // alternate function
               | (GPIO_ALTERNATE << GPIO_MODER_MODE8_Pos);
  GPIOB->AFR[1] = ((GPIOB->AFR[1]) & ~GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL8_Msk)   // ... which one:
                | (GPIO_AF6 << GPIO_AFRH_AFSEL8_Pos);         // AF6
        // ... the same two pairs again for PB9 ...
  GPIOB->OTYPER |= (GPIO_OTYPER_OT8 | GPIO_OTYPER_OT9);    // open drain

  RCC->APBENR1 |= RCC_APBENR1_I2C1EN;   // clock to I2C1
  I2C1->CR1 &= ~I2C_CR1_PE;             // TIMINGR is writable only when
  I2C1->TIMINGR &= 0x0;                 //   the peripheral is disabled
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0x2 << I2C_TIMINGR_PRESC_Pos);
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0x4 << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLDEL_Pos) | (0x2 << I2C_TIMINGR_SDADEL_Pos);
  I2C1->TIMINGR |= (0xf << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLH_Pos) | (0x13 << I2C_TIMINGR_SCLL_Pos);
  I2C1->CR1 |= I2C_CR1_PE;              // ... and enable again
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li><c>AFR[1]</c>, not <c>AFR[0]</c>, for the same reason <c>EXTICR[1]</c> was not <c>EXTICR[2]</c> this week: two registers, pins 0&#8211;7 and pins 8&#8211;15. The manual calls the second one <c>GPIOx_AFRH</c>.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Abridged: PB9's MODER and AFR lines are dropped so the function fits legibly. The book carries the full version — say so.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-library">
            <title>Part 7a: What an I2C Library Has to Provide</title>
            <p>
                You have now watched the protocol run twice.  Before looking at
                the library you were handed, work out what has to be in it.  A
                program using a device on this bus needs a small
                number of operations, and you can derive the list from what you
                just saw on the screen rather than from anybody's API.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-five-operations">
                <title>What Five Things Does the Library Have to Do?</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Write your list down before we compare.  Think about
                        what a transaction consists of and what a program has
                        to be able to do with a device.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-five-t1">
                    <statement><p>List the operations a program needs in order
                    to use this bus.  Aim for the smallest set that covers
                    everything you saw on the trace, and say for each one why it
                    cannot be built out of the others.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-five-t1b">
                    <statement><p>Then take your own capture and check the list
                    against it: which of your operations produced the START, the
                    address, the ACK, the data byte and the STOP?  Looking only
                    at the trace, could you tell a one-byte write from a
                    multi-byte one?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-five-t2">
                    <statement><p>One of them has nothing to do with any
                    particular device.  Which, and what does it have to set
                    up?</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                The five, in the order Petra's own list gives them.  There is
                <term>initialization</term>, which happens once and belongs to
                the bus rather than to any device: claim the pins, set the clock
                rate, switch the peripheral on.  Then <term>write one byte</term>
                to a device and <term>read one byte</term> from it, which is
                everything the ping did.  Then <term>write several bytes</term>
                and <term>read several bytes</term> in a single transaction — and
                those two carry one extra argument, because a device with more
                than one thing inside it needs to be told <em>where</em> the
                bytes are going.  In C:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* Configure I2C pins, initialize bus speed */
void i2c1_init(void);

/* Write one byte to target */
void i2c1_byteWrite(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t data);

/* Read one byte from a target */
void i2c1_byteRead(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t *data);

/* Write one or more bytes to a memory location within target */
void i2c1_memWrite(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t registerAddr,
                   uint8_t nbytes, uint8_t *data);

/* Read one or more bytes from a memory location within target */
void i2c1_memRead(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t registerAddr,
                  uint8_t nbytes, uint8_t *data);
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Why <q>several bytes in one transaction</q> is a separate
                function and not a loop over the single-byte one: between two
                calls to <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> the bus goes back to idle, with
                a STOP after the first and a fresh START before the second.
                Some devices treat that as two unrelated instructions.  On
                You are about to meet one where it matters.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you have used I2C on an Arduino, this is the library you were
                using.  <c>Wire.begin()</c> is <c>i2c1_init()</c>.
                <c>Wire.beginTransmission(addr)</c>, a <c>Wire.write()</c> and
                <c>Wire.endTransmission()</c> together are one
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> — or, with more than one
                <c>Wire.write()</c> between them, one <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c>.
                <c>Wire.requestFrom(addr, n)</c> followed by <c>Wire.read()</c>
                is <c>i2c1_byteRead()</c> — but <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> is
                <em>not</em> that.  Reading a numbered register on an Arduino
                takes both halves: <c>beginTransmission(addr)</c>,
                <c>write(regAddr)</c>, <c>endTransmission(false)</c> — the
                <c>false</c> being a repeated START rather than a STOP — and only
                then <c>requestFrom()</c>.  Get that wrong and you read whatever
                byte the device streams next rather than the register you asked
                for; <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-transfer-pattern"/> draws exactly
                this.  One more: <c>Wire.begin()</c> is overloaded — with no
                argument it is the controller-mode setup above, and with an
                address it makes the board a <em>target</em>, a mode this course
                never uses.
            </p>
            <p>
                The addresses you passed to <c>Wire</c> were 7-bit addresses,
                exactly like the ones here.  What is <em>not</em> the same is
                what you get back.  <c>Wire.endTransmission()</c> returns a status
                byte as soon as the result is available;
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> returns nothing at all and, as you found twenty minutes ago, can
                hang instead.  That is the bug you have already met, and it is the
                honest version of <q>this is the same library</q>.  The other
                difference is that this time you can open the file and see what
                happens underneath.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-five-ops">
    <p><term>The five, and why five.</term> Yours may be worded differently; check it covers the same ground.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void i2c1_init(void);                                     // pins, bus speed

void i2c1_byteWrite(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t data);    // one byte out
void i2c1_byteRead (uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t *data);   // one byte in

void i2c1_memWrite(uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t registerAddr,
                   uint8_t nbytes, uint8_t *data);        // several bytes out
void i2c1_memRead (uint8_t deviceAddr, uint8_t registerAddr,
                   uint8_t nbytes, uint8_t *data);        // several bytes in
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li><term>Initialization</term> happens once and belongs to the bus, not to any device: claim the pins, set the clock rate, switch the peripheral on.</li>
        <li><term>One byte out</term> and <term>one byte in</term> are everything the ping did.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-five-why">
    <p><term>Why the last two are separate functions and not loops.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><term>Several bytes</term> each way carry one extra argument, <c>registerAddr</c>, because a device with more than one thing inside it has to be told <em>where</em> the bytes are going.</li>
        <li>And between two calls to <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> the bus <term>goes idle</term> &#8212; a STOP after the first, a fresh START before the second. Some devices treat that as two unrelated instructions rather than one longer one.</li>
        <li>The display you have been writing to is one of them. Sending all ten of its bytes in one transaction is not an optimization; it is the only thing that works.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-wire-h">
    <p><term>If you have used I2C on an Arduino, this is the library you were using.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
Wire.begin();                                 ->  i2c1_init();

Wire.beginTransmission(addr);                 ->  i2c1_byteWrite(addr, b);
Wire.write(b);
Wire.endTransmission();

... with several Wire.write()s between them   ->  i2c1_memWrite(addr, reg, n, data);

Wire.requestFrom(addr, 1);                    ->  i2c1_byteRead(addr, &b);
b = Wire.read();
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The addresses you passed to <c>Wire</c> were <term>7-bit</term> addresses, exactly like these.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-wire-h-gaps">
    <p><term>Two places that mapping is not one-to-one</term> &#8212; and both of them matter.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
Wire.beginTransmission(addr);   // reading a NUMBERED REGISTER takes both halves
Wire.write(regAddr);
Wire.endTransmission(false);    // false = repeated START, not a STOP
Wire.requestFrom(addr, n);      // ... and only THEN this
b = Wire.read();                //  ->  i2c1_memRead(addr, reg, n, data);

uint8_t err = Wire.endTransmission();     // returns 0..4 as soon as it can
i2c1_byteWrite(addr, data);               // returns void, and can hang
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>A bare <c>requestFrom</c> is <c>i2c1_byteRead()</c>, <term>not</term> <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>. Get that wrong and you read whatever byte the device streams next rather than the register you asked for.</li>
        <li>And <c>endTransmission()</c> hands back a status byte where <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> hands back nothing and can hang &#8212; which is the bug you met twenty minutes ago.</li>
        <li>What you gain is that this time you can open the file and see what happens underneath.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day9x-bytewrite">
            <title>Part 7b: Opening i2c1_byteWrite()</title>
            <p>
                Five functions is the interface.  This is the one that produced
                the trace you captured forty minutes ago, and it is short enough
                to read in full.  Here is its sending half:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    // Ping target address for writing 1 byte, use "=" to ensure all other bits are cleared
    I2C1->CR2  = (deviceAddr<<1)
                 | (1U << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos)  // sending one byte only
                 | I2C_CR2_AUTOEND             // Send Stop condition after transfer
                 | I2C_CR2_START;              // Send Start condition
    while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_BUSY) );      // Wait until Start is received

    // Send the data
    if( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_NACKF) ) {       // Make sure ACK received
        while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_TXIS) );  // Wait for TX to be ready
        I2C1->TXDR = data;                     // put data in send register
    }
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Five fields go into <c>I2C_CR2</c> in that one assignment, and
                between them they describe the entire transaction before any of
                it happens: <em>who</em> (the address, shifted left by one into
                the <c>SADD</c> field), <em>which direction</em>
                (<c>RD_WRN</c>, left at 0 here, which means write),
                <em>how many bytes</em> (<c>NBYTES</c>), <em>what to do at the
                end</em> (<c>AUTOEND</c>, which makes the hardware send the STOP
                on its own), and <em>go</em> (<c>START</c>).  Writing that last
                bit is what puts the START condition on the wire; everything you
                watched on the oscilloscope followed from this one line.
            </p>
            <p>
                Notice the <c>=</c>, and the comment on it.  The habit from the
                timer chapter —
                assign a whole mask rather than compound-assigning one bit
                (<xref ref="subsec-day8-rcw0"/>) — applies here for a different
                reason than it did there.  On a status register the danger was
                what the read-back would clear.  <c>I2C_CR2</c> is a control
                register and reads back fine; the danger is what is
                <em>left over</em>.  The address, the direction and the byte
                count from the previous transaction are all still sitting in it,
                and <c>|=</c> cannot clear a bit — so an <c>|=</c> here would
                leave the old address ORed with the new one.  Assigning the whole
                mask states the transaction completely, every time.
            </p>
            <p>
                The two waits are the peripheral talking back.
                <c>I2C_ISR_BUSY</c> comes up when the START has actually
                appeared on the bus, and <c>I2C_ISR_TXIS</c> — transmit
                interrupt status — comes up when the transmit register is empty
                and the hardware is ready for the byte, which happens after the
                address has been sent and acknowledged.
                <xref ref="fig-i2c-master-tx"/> is the reference manual's own
                picture of that sequence, with the flags on the timeline where
                they occur.  Reading a byte is the same shape with three changes:
                <c>I2C_CR2_RD_WRN</c> set, a wait on <c>I2C_ISR_RXNE</c> instead
                of <c>TXIS</c>, and <c>*data = I2C1->RXDR;</c> instead of the
                write.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-master-tx">
                <caption>RM0490's transfer diagram for a controller writing two
                bytes with automatic end mode, with the parts named.  The setup
                step programs the address, the byte count and AUTOEND and sets
                START; the hardware then puts the START condition, the address
                and the R/W bit on the wire and collects the ACK.  TXIS rises
                once per byte, each time the transmit data register is free —
                that is the flag <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> waits on before writing
                <c>I2C1->TXDR</c>.  TXE below it is the same information as a
                level rather than an event.  NBYTES holds the programmed count —
                2 for the whole of this transfer — and once that many bytes have
                gone, AUTOEND puts the STOP on the wire without any further help
                from the program.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_master_tx_sequence.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Everything below <c>I2C1->CR2</c> is done by a state machine in
                silicon.  Nothing in this library counts clock edges or times a
                pulse; it sets up a transaction, and the peripheral shifts the
                bits out, generates the START and the STOP, releases SDA for the
                ACK slot and reports what came back.  What is left for software
                is describing the transaction and reading the flags —
                <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-hardware"/> has the peripheral's own
                block diagram, if you want to see what that state machine is made
                of.
            </p>




            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-bytewrite">
    <p><term>The sending half of i2c1_byteWrite(), in full:</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    // use "=" to ensure all other bits are cleared
    I2C1->CR2  = (deviceAddr<<1)
                 | (1U << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos)  // sending one byte only
                 | I2C_CR2_AUTOEND             // Stop condition after transfer
                 | I2C_CR2_START;              // Send Start condition
    while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_BUSY) );      // Wait until Start is received

    if( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_NACKF) ) {       // Make sure ACK received
        while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_TXIS) );  // Wait for TX to be ready
        I2C1->TXDR = data;                     // put data in send register
    }
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Five fields describe the <term>entire transaction before any of it happens</term>: <em>who</em> (the address, shifted into SADD), <em>which direction</em> (RD_WRN, 0 here), <em>how many bytes</em> (NBYTES), <em>what to do at the end</em> (AUTOEND), and <em>go</em> (START).</li>
        <li>Writing that last bit is what puts the START on the wire. Everything you watched on the oscilloscope followed from this one line.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-cr2-assign">
    <p><term>Notice the <c>=</c>, and the comment on it.</term> The habit from the timers chapter, for a different reason than it had there.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
I2C1->CR2  = (deviceAddr<<1) | ... ;    // states the whole transaction
I2C1->CR2 |= (deviceAddr<<1) | ... ;    // ORs it onto the LAST one
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>On a <term>status</term> register the danger was what the read-back would clear. <c>I2C_CR2</c> is a <term>control</term> register and reads back fine.</li>
        <li>The danger here is what is <term>left over</term>: the address, the direction and the byte count from the previous transaction are all still in there, and <c>|=</c> cannot clear a bit. An <c>|=</c> leaves the old address ORed with the new one.</li>
        <li>Assigning the whole mask states the transaction completely, every time.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day9x-master-tx" ref="fig-i2c-master-tx" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>The two waits are the peripheral talking back. <c>BUSY</c> comes up when the START has actually appeared on the bus; <c>TXIS</c> comes up when the transmit register is empty and the hardware is ready for the byte &#8212; <term>after the address has been sent and acknowledged</term>.</li>
        <li>Reading a byte is the same shape with three changes: <c>I2C_CR2_RD_WRN</c> set, a wait on <c>RXNE</c> instead of <c>TXIS</c>, and <c>*data = I2C1-&gt;RXDR;</c> instead of the write.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Set-up, then the hardware does the START, the address and the ACK by itself; TXIS signals when each byte can go.</caption>
    <note>The point to make: nothing in the library counts a clock edge or times a pulse. It describes a transaction and reads flags; a state machine in silicon does the rest. Once NBYTES bytes have been transferred, AUTOEND issues the STOP.</note>
</slide>

        </subsection>


        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-layers">
            <title>Part 8a: Layers, and What You Are Replacing</title>
            <p>
                Stand back from the code for a moment
                (<xref ref="fig-firmware-layers"/>).  Underneath everything are
                the machine registers — <c>I2C1->CR2</c>, <c>GPIOB->MODER</c> —
                which belong to the STM32C031C6 and to nothing else.  Above them
                sit the <term>interface drivers</term>: <c>uart.c</c>,
                <c>adc.c</c>, <c>i2c.c</c>.  Those are the only files in the
                project allowed to touch a machine register, and each one turns
                one peripheral into a handful of functions.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-firmware-layers">
                <caption>The four layers of a firmware design, with the two
                middle ones labeled by their rule as well as their name.  At the
                bottom, the microcontroller's own registers.  Above them the
                interface drivers, the only code that reaches a machine register:
                <c>uart.c</c> and <c>i2c.c</c> here, with <c>stdio.c</c> sitting
                on <c>uart.c</c> so that <c>printf()</c> works.  Above those the
                device drivers, which reach a device's registers only through an
                interface driver and never touch a machine register — the layer
                you are writing today, over the I2C bus, for a device with its own
                registers at the far end of it.  And on top, <c>main()</c>, which
                calls devices through their drivers and knows about neither kind
                of register.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day10-I2C(2)/firmware_layers.svg" width="85%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Above <em>those</em> are <term>device drivers</term>, and that is
                the layer with the interesting rule.  A device driver knows about
                one device: its commands, its addresses, its quirks.  It does not
                know how the bytes get there.  <c>SevenSeg.c</c> may call
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> as often as it likes and may not touch
                <c>I2C1->CR2</c> even once.  The point is not tidiness — it is
                that the same display driver runs unchanged on any board whose
                I2C library offers those five functions, and that the day you
                move to a different microcontroller you rewrite <c>i2c.c</c> and
                nothing else.
            </p>
            <p>
                On top is <c>main()</c>, which calls
                <c>SevenSeg_write()</c> and has no idea there is a bus involved
                at all.
            </p>
            <p>
                If you have driven one of these displays from an Arduino, you
                have used somebody else's version of the layer you are about to
                write.  <c>Adafruit_LEDBackpack.h</c> is a device driver for this
                exact chip: <c>begin(0x70)</c> is <c>SevenSeg_init()</c>,
                <c>setBrightness()</c> is <c>SevenSeg_dim()</c>,
                <c>blinkRate()</c> is <c>SevenSeg_blink()</c>, and
                <c>writeDisplay()</c> is <c>SevenSeg_write()</c>.  Underneath, it
                calls <c>Wire</c>, which is <c>i2c.c</c>.  The same four
                functions and the same two layers, written by somebody who read
                the same datasheet pages you read in Part 4.
            </p>
            <p>
                What writing it yourself buys is not performance.  It is that
                when the display shows nothing, you have a bus you can put a
                scope on, a command table you can check the bytes against, and
                four functions you can read — instead of a library whose failure
                mode is that it did not work.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-layers" ref="fig-firmware-layers" stack="yes">
    <ul>
        <li>Bottom the <term>machine registers</term>; above them the <term>interface drivers</term>, the only files allowed to touch one.</li>
        <li>Then the <term>device drivers</term>, and on top <c>main()</c>, which has no idea there is a bus involved.</li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Today you write the third layer up — the first one in this course that has been yours.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-layers-rule">
    <p><term>The device-driver layer is the one with the rule.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><c>SevenSeg.c</c> knows <term>one device</term> &#8212; its commands, its addresses, its quirks &#8212; and does not know how the bytes get there. It may call <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> as often as it likes, and may not touch <c>I2C1-&gt;CR2</c> once.</li>
        <li>The point is not tidiness. The same display driver runs <term>unchanged</term> on any board whose I2C library offers those five functions.</li>
        <li>And the day you move to a different microcontroller, you rewrite <c>i2c.c</c> and nothing else. That is what the rule buys.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-adafruit">
    <p><term>If you have driven one of these from an Arduino, you have used somebody else's version of the layer you are about to write.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
Adafruit_LEDBackpack.h                    SevenSeg.h

  begin(0x70)                 ->            SevenSeg_init()
  setBrightness(b)            ->            SevenSeg_dim(b)
  blinkRate(r)                ->            SevenSeg_blink(r)
  writeDisplay()              ->            SevenSeg_write(buffer)

        ... and underneath, it calls Wire, which is i2c.c
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The same four functions and the same two layers, written by somebody who read the same datasheet pages you read in Part 4.</li>
        <li>What writing it yourself buys is not performance. It is that when the display shows nothing you have <term>a bus you can put a scope on</term>, a command table you can check the bytes against, and four functions you can read &#8212; instead of a library whose failure mode is that it did not work.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-first-digit">
            <title>Part 8b: SevenSeg_init(), and One Digit</title>
            <p>
                You put a character on a digit yesterday, using a program that
                had the three setup commands written into it.  Now write those
                three yourself, as the first function of the driver, and prove
                them with the smallest program that needs them.
                <c>writeFirstDigit.c</c> is that program.  It is given to you,
                and it will do nothing at all until
                <c>SevenSeg_init()</c> exists.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#include "i2c.h"
#include "SevenSegPartial.h"
int main(void) {
    uint8_t display_addr = HT16K33_ADDR;
    uint8_t display_subaddr = 0;       // 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 - depending on the digit
    uint8_t digit_data = 0b01111001;   // 'E'
    i2c1_init();
    SevenSeg_init();

    while(1) {
        i2c1_memWrite(display_addr, display_subaddr, 1, &digit_data);
        delay_ms(250);
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Three arguments of that <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> call are worth
                naming, because they are the same three every time.
                <c>display_addr</c> is who — the device on the bus.
                <c>display_subaddr</c> is where inside it — the display RAM
                address the pointer starts at.  And <c>1</c> is how many bytes
                follow, with <c>&amp;digit_data</c> being where to get them.
                The <c>0b01111001</c> is an <c>E</c>: bits 0, 3, 4, 5 and 6, so
                <c>a d e f g</c>.
            </p>
            <p>
                It also calls <c>SevenSeg_init()</c>, which does not exist yet.
                <c>SevenSegPartial.c</c> is the file your driver goes in, and it
                arrives as four empty functions with their signatures already
                written.  Fill in the first one now; the other three are
                <xref ref="subsec-day10-driver"/>.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#include "i2c.h"
#include "SevenSegPartial.h"

// Initialize the display: oscillator on, display on and steady, full brightness
void SevenSeg_init() {
    // TODO 1 -- three one-byte writes, in this order: system setup with the
    //           oscillator on, display setup with the display on and not
    //           blinking, and dimming set to full brightness.  Build each
    //           byte out of the names in SevenSegPartial.h.

}

// Change the display flashing
void SevenSeg_blink(uint8_t rate) {
    // TODO 2 -- one byte: the display-setup command, the display-on bit, and
    //           the caller's rate masked to the two blink bits.

}

// Dim the display
void SevenSeg_dim(uint8_t brightness) {
    // TODO 3 -- one byte: the dimming command with the caller's brightness
    //           masked to the four brightness bits.

}

// Write the display RAM
void SevenSeg_write(uint8_t *display_buffer) {
    // display_buffer is size 2*HT16K33_NBUF (high byte, low byte)
    // TODO 4 -- one call.  Every byte in ONE transaction, starting at display
    //           RAM address HT16K33_ADDR_PTR.  Do not touch an I2C register
    //           directly -- go through the i2c library.

}
]]></code></program>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-first-digit">
                <title>Write One Digit, Then Another</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        <c>SevenSegPartial.c</c>, <c>SevenSegPartial.h</c> and
                        <c>writeFirstDigit.c</c> are on Canvas.  Copy
                        <c>TemplateProject</c> to a project named
                        <c>SevenSegI2CSecondSteps</c>; the two
                        <c>SevenSegPartial</c> files go in <c>Src</c> and
                        <c>Inc</c>, and <c>writeFirstDigit.c</c> goes in
                        <c>Src</c>.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-first-t1">
                    <statement><p>Fill in TODO 1, <c>SevenSeg_init()</c>, with
                    the three commands you derived in Part 4 — written as the
                    header's names, not as 0x21, 0x81 and 0xEF.  Build, flash,
                    and confirm the first digit shows an <c>E</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-first-t2">
                    <statement><p>Move it: change one line so the
                    <c>E</c> appears on the second digit, then the third, then
                    the fourth.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-first-t3">
                    <statement><p>Change what it says: make the digit show an
                    <c>S</c>, then a <c>2</c>, then an <c>8</c>.  Derive each
                    pattern from <xref ref="fig-segment-map"/> rather than
                    guessing.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-first-t4">
                    <statement><p>Set <c>display_subaddr</c> to 1 and predict,
                    before you flash it, what the display will do.  Then try
                    it.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <c>SevenSeg_init()</c>, filled in:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void SevenSeg_init() {
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD | HT16K33_OSC_ON);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON | HT16K33_BLINK_OFF);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD | 0xf);
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Three transactions, not one: each is a separate command, and the
                HT16K33 wants each one addressed to it in its own right.  Setting
                the oscillator has to come first — until it is running the chip
                accepts commands and drives nothing.
            </p>
            <p>
                <em>Nothing lights up?</em>  The bus was proved in Part 2, so the
                fault is above it.  Check that <c>SevenSeg_init()</c> is actually
                called, and that its first command is the one that turns the
                oscillator on: with the oscillator off the HT16K33 accepts
                everything you send it and drives nothing at all, which looks
                exactly like a display that is not wired.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-skeleton">
    <p><term>writeFirstDigit.c</term>, given. One byte into one address of the display RAM, over and over.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
int main(void) {
    uint8_t display_addr = HT16K33_ADDR;
    uint8_t display_subaddr = 0;      // 0, 2, 4, 6, 8 - depending on the digit
    uint8_t digit_data = 0b01111001;  // 'E'
    i2c1_init();
    SevenSeg_init();
    while(1) {
        i2c1_memWrite(display_addr, display_subaddr, 1, &digit_data);
        delay_ms(250);
    }
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Three of <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c>'s arguments are the same three every time: <c>display_addr</c> is <term>who</term>, <c>display_subaddr</c> is <term>where inside it</term>, and the <c>1</c> is <term>how many bytes follow</term>.</li>
        <li><c>0b01111001</c> sets bits 0, 3, 4, 5 and 6 &#8212; segments <c>a d e f g</c>, an <c>E</c>.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-skeleton-init">
    <p><term>It calls SevenSeg_init(), which does not exist yet.</term> <c>SevenSegPartial.c</c> is the file your driver goes in: four empty functions, signatures already written. Fill in the first; the other three are Part 8c.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void SevenSeg_init() {
    // TODO 1 -- three one-byte writes, in this order: system setup with the
    //           oscillator on, display setup with the display on and not
    //           blinking, and dimming set to full brightness.  Build each
    //           byte out of the names in SevenSegPartial.h.

}
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Then move the <c>E</c>: digit 2, 3, 4. Then change it: <c>S</c>, <c>2</c>, <c>8</c>, derived from the segment map rather than guessed.</li>
        <li>Then set <c>display_subaddr</c> to 1 and <term>predict what happens before you flash it</term>.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-init-answer">
    <p><term>SevenSeg_init(), filled in.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void SevenSeg_init() {
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_SYSTEM_CMD | HT16K33_OSC_ON);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON
                                                     | HT16K33_BLINK_OFF);
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD | 0xf);
}
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Three transactions, not one: each is a separate command, and the HT16K33 wants each one addressed to it in its own right.</li>
        <li>The oscillator goes first. Until it is running the chip accepts commands and drives nothing &#8212; which looks exactly like a display that is not wired.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-driver">
            <title>Part 8c: The Rest of the Driver</title>
            <p>
                <c>SevenSegPartial.c</c> has three functions left in it, and
                their prototypes are already in the header.  Two are warm-ups
                that rehearse the one-byte write you used in
                <c>SevenSeg_init()</c>; the third is the one that puts the whole
                display up, and it is the crucial step of the session.
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void SevenSeg_init();                           // Initialize the display
void SevenSeg_blink(uint8_t rate);              // Blink the display
void SevenSeg_dim(uint8_t brightness);          // Dim the display
void SevenSeg_write(uint8_t *display_buffer);   // Write the display RAM
void SevenSeg_number(uint16_t num, uint8_t *display_buffer);
                                                // Convert a number to segments
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                <c>SevenSeg_blink()</c> and <c>SevenSeg_dim()</c> are each one
                call to <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c>, sending the display-setup and
                dimming commands from Part 4 with the caller's option bits ORed
                in.  Mask the argument to the bits the command actually has —
                <c>0x6</c> for the two blink bits, <c>0xf</c> for the four
                brightness bits — so that a caller passing something out of range
                cannot corrupt the command itself.
            </p>
            <p>
                <c>SevenSeg_write()</c> is where the RAM map from Part 5 turns
                into code.  It receives a pointer to the caller's ten-byte
                buffer, and it has to get all ten into the display RAM starting
                at address 0, in one transaction.  Everything needed for that is
                in <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c>'s four arguments; the function is one
                line.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-sevenseg">
                <title>Finish the Driver and Show ES.28</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        In <c>SevenSegPartial.c</c>.  The header defines
                        <c>HT16K33_ADDR</c>, the command names from Part 4,
                        <c>HT16K33_ADDR_PTR</c> for the display RAM's starting
                        address, and <c>HT16K33_NBUF</c> for the number of
                        digit-and-colon positions, so the buffer is
                        <c>2*HT16K33_NBUF</c> bytes.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-sevenseg-t1">
                    <statement><p>Write <c>SevenSeg_blink()</c> and
                    <c>SevenSeg_dim()</c>.  One line each.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-sevenseg-t2">
                    <statement><p>Write <c>SevenSeg_write()</c>.  Before you
                    write it, say out loud what each of
                    <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c>'s four arguments has to be and
                    why.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-sevenseg-t3">
                    <statement><p>In a new <c>main()</c>, declare
                    <c>uint8_t display_buffer[2*HT16K33_NBUF];</c>, fill it so
                    the display reads <c>ES.28</c>, and call
                    <c>SevenSeg_write()</c>.  The <c>E</c> and the <c>S.</c> are
                    bytes you already worked out — open your
                    <c>writeFirstDigit.c</c> and copy them rather than deriving
                    them a second time; for the <c>2</c> and the <c>8</c> use
                    <c>numbertable[]</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-sevenseg-t4">
                    <statement><p>Done early?  Make the display do a sequence:
                    <c>ES.28</c> steady at full brightness, wait a second, blink
                    at 1 Hz, wait five seconds, then steady again at a lower
                    brightness.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                The three functions, complete:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Change the display flashing
void SevenSeg_blink(uint8_t rate) {
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON | (rate & 0x6));
}

// Dim the display
void SevenSeg_dim(uint8_t brightness) {
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD | (brightness & 0xf));
}

// Write the display RAM
void SevenSeg_write(uint8_t *display_buffer) {
    // display_buffer is size 2*HT16K33_NBUF (high byte, low byte)
    i2c1_memWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_ADDR_PTR, 2*HT16K33_NBUF, display_buffer);
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                And the program that drives them, which is the answer to the last
                two tasks:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "ES28.h"
#include "i2c.h"
#include "SevenSegPartial.h"
int main(void) {
    uint8_t display_buffer[2*HT16K33_NBUF];
    i2c1_init();
    SevenSeg_init();
    SevenSeg_dim(15);
    display_buffer[0] = 0b01111001;     // E
    display_buffer[1] = 0;
    display_buffer[2] = 0b11101101;     // S.
    display_buffer[3] = 0;
    display_buffer[4] = 0;              // no colon
    display_buffer[5] = 0;
    display_buffer[6] = numbertable[2]; // 2
    display_buffer[7] = 0;
    display_buffer[8] = numbertable[8]; // 8
    display_buffer[9] = 0;
    SevenSeg_write(display_buffer);
    delay_ms(1000);
    SevenSeg_blink(HT16K33_BLINK_1HZ);
    delay_ms(5000);
    SevenSeg_blink(HT16K33_BLINK_OFF);
    SevenSeg_dim(5);
    return 1;
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Three things about that listing.  The odd-numbered bytes are the
                zeros from Part 5, and they are what move the pointer along.
                <c>0b11101101</c> is an <c>S</c> with bit 7 set, which is the
                decimal point — one buffer entry produces both.  And this
                <c>main()</c> has no <c>while(1)</c>: it runs once, sets the
                display, and stops.  The display keeps showing <c>ES.28</c>
                afterwards, because the HT16K33 is still sweeping its RAM whether
                or not the STM32C031C6 is doing anything at all.  That is the
                whole argument for the backpack, visible on your desk.
            </p>
            <p>
                <em>Nothing, or the wrong thing, on the display?</em>  Work down
                the layers rather than around them.  Does
                <c>writeFirstDigit.c</c> still put an <c>E</c> up?  If yes, the
                bus, the init and the RAM addressing are all fine and the fault
                is in <c>SevenSeg_write()</c> or in how the buffer was filled.
                A display showing four garbled characters usually means the
                buffer is right but shifted — check that you sent
                <c>2*HT16K33_NBUF</c> bytes and started at
                <c>HT16K33_ADDR_PTR</c>, not that you got the segment patterns
                wrong.  And if you have been through all of that and still have
                nothing, flag it — we're here to help.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-driver-todo">
    <p><term>Three functions left, and their prototypes are already in the header.</term> Two rehearse the one-byte write you used in <c>SevenSeg_init()</c>. The third puts the whole display up, and it is today's crucial step.</p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void SevenSeg_blink(uint8_t rate) {
    // TODO 2 -- one byte: display-setup command, display-on bit, rate
}

void SevenSeg_dim(uint8_t brightness) {
    // TODO 3 -- one byte: the dimming command, plus the brightness
}

void SevenSeg_write(uint8_t *display_buffer) {
    // TODO 4 -- ONE call.  Every byte in one transaction, from
    //           HT16K33_ADDR_PTR.  Go through the i2c library.
}
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>Mask each argument to the bits its command actually has &#8212; <c>0x6</c> for blink, <c>0xf</c> for brightness &#8212; so a caller passing something out of range cannot corrupt the command itself.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-driver-answer">
    <p><term>The three functions.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
// Change the display flashing
void SevenSeg_blink(uint8_t rate) {
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_DISPLAY_ON | (rate & 0x6));
}

// Dim the display
void SevenSeg_dim(uint8_t brightness) {
    i2c1_byteWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD | (brightness & 0xf));
}

// Write the display RAM
void SevenSeg_write(uint8_t *display_buffer) {
    // display_buffer is size 2*HT16K33_NBUF (high byte, low byte)
    i2c1_memWrite(HT16K33_ADDR, HT16K33_ADDR_PTR, 2*HT16K33_NBUF, display_buffer);
}
]]></code></program>
    <caption>SevenSeg_write() is one line, because Part 5 did the understanding.</caption>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-es28" room="compressed">
    <p><term>ES.28, out of a ten-byte buffer.</term></p>
    <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    uint8_t display_buffer[2*HT16K33_NBUF];
    i2c1_init();  SevenSeg_init();  SevenSeg_dim(15);
    display_buffer[0] = 0b01111001;      display_buffer[1] = 0;   // E
    display_buffer[2] = 0b11101101;      display_buffer[3] = 0;   // S.
    display_buffer[4] = 0;               display_buffer[5] = 0;   // no colon
    display_buffer[6] = numbertable[2];  display_buffer[7] = 0;   // 2
    display_buffer[8] = numbertable[8];  display_buffer[9] = 0;   // 8
    SevenSeg_write(display_buffer);
    delay_ms(1000);   SevenSeg_blink(HT16K33_BLINK_1HZ);
    delay_ms(5000);   SevenSeg_blink(HT16K33_BLINK_OFF);   SevenSeg_dim(5);
]]></code></program>
    <ul>
        <li>The odd bytes are Part 5's zeros, and they are what move the pointer along. <c>0b11101101</c> is an <c>S</c> with bit 7 set &#8212; a letter and its decimal point in one entry.</li>
        <li><term>No <c>while(1)</c>.</term> It runs once and stops, and the display keeps showing <c>ES.28</c> &#8212; the HT16K33 is still sweeping its RAM whether or not the STM32C031C6 is doing anything. The argument for the backpack, on your desk.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-ladder">
    <p><term>Nothing, or the wrong thing, on the display?</term> Work down the layers rather than around them.</p>
    <ul>
        <li>1 &#8212; Does <c>writeFirstDigit.c</c> still put an <c>E</c> up? If yes, the bus, the init and the RAM addressing are all fine and the fault is in <c>SevenSeg_write()</c> or in how the buffer was filled.</li>
        <li>2 &#8212; Four garbled characters usually means the buffer is right but <term>shifted</term>: check you sent <c>2*HT16K33_NBUF</c> bytes and started at <c>HT16K33_ADDR_PTR</c>, before suspecting the segment patterns.</li>
        <li>3 &#8212; Nothing at all, and <c>writeFirstDigit.c</c> also shows nothing: back to Part 2. The bus, then the oscillator.</li>
        <li>4 &#8212; Been through all three and still nothing? <term>Flag it. We're here to help!</term></li>
    </ul>
    <caption>Each step rules out a layer — start at the one you know worked.</caption>
    <note>Stays up for all of Part 8c.</note>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-breakit">
            <title>Part 9: Break It on Purpose</title>
            <p>
                Two one-line changes to a program that works, and a capture of
                each.  The point is not either failure on its own; it is that
                they look completely different on the wire and identical on the
                display, which is what makes a scope worth reaching for.
            </p>
            <p>
                Put the AD2 back on SDA and SCL as you had it yesterday, in
                oscilloscope mode, and work on a copy of
                <c>helloDisplay.c</c> so that your driver project stays intact.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-scope-noack">
                <caption>One I2C transaction that got no answer, with SDA in
                blue above and SCL in orange below.  The decoded bits are marked
                on the capture: eight clock pulses carry
                1 1 0 0 0 0 0 followed by 0 — the 7-bit address 0x60 and a write
                — and on the ninth pulse SDA stays HIGH, which is the
                <c>NoAck</c>.  No device on this bus has address 0x60.  Nine
                clock pulses in all against the eighteen of
                <xref ref="fig-i2c-scope-ack"/>, and then the bus is
                idle.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/scope_ping_noack.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-wrong-address">
                <title>Two Ways to Get a Blank Display</title>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-wrong-t1">
                    <statement><p>Change <c>HT16K33_ADDR</c> to <c>0x60</c>,
                    rebuild, flash, and capture a single sweep.  You will have to
                    arm the sweep first and then press the Nucleo's black reset
                    button — the paragraph after this activity says why.  Find
                    the NACK and the STOP, and check the capture against
                    <xref ref="fig-i2c-scope-noack"/>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-wrong-t4">
                    <statement><p>Put the address back to <c>0x70</c>.  Now
                    comment out the <em>first</em> of the three command writes —
                    the system-setup one that turns the oscillator on — and
                    predict, in writing, what you will see on the display and
                    what you will see on the trace.  Then flash it and
                    capture.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-wrong-t5">
                    <statement><p>Compare the two captures and the two displays.
                    Which of the two faults could you have diagnosed from the
                    display alone, and which needed the scope?  Which could you
                    have diagnosed from the scope alone?</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-wrong-t6">
                    <statement><p>Done early?  Leave the oscillator command
                    commented out and add it back at the <em>end</em> of the
                    three, after the display-on and brightness commands.  Does
                    the display come back?  Say what that tells you about
                    whether the HT16K33 stores a command it cannot act on
                    yet.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                <em>The wrong address.</em>  With <c>0x60</c> the program stops
                after one transaction rather than repeating four times a second,
                so there is nothing for the scope to catch unless you arm it and
                then restart the board.  The reason is in the library, and it is
                worth knowing before you meet it in Lab 5.
                <c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> checks for a NACK once, immediately after
                setting START — long before the ninth clock pulse, so the check
                always passes — and then waits for the peripheral to say it is
                ready for data.  RM0490 §23.4.9 is explicit that on a NACK
                <q>the TXIS flag is not set</q>, so that wait never ends.  The
                hardware still does the right thing on the wire — the same
                paragraph goes on to say that after a NACK <q>a STOP condition is
                automatically sent</q>, which is why the capture looks tidy.  It
                is the program that is stuck.
                <xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-library"/> shows the flow the reference
                manual gives for doing it without that hole.
            </p>
            <p>
                <em>The missing oscillator command.</em>  This one is the
                interesting case, and it is the reason this part exists.  The
                display is blank — the same blank as a wrong address, the same
                blank as a wire that fell out.  The trace is
                <term>perfect</term>: every transaction is there, every address
                byte is 0xE0, every ninth pulse comes back LOW because the
                HT16K33 is powered, listening, and acknowledging every single
                byte you send it.  It simply is not driving anything, because
                nothing told its oscillator to run.
            </p>
            <p>
                So the bus can be flawless and the device can still do nothing,
                and no amount of staring at SDA and SCL will tell you that.  A
                scope answers <q>did my bytes arrive?</q> and answers it
                completely.  It cannot answer <q>were they the right bytes?</q>
                — that question is a datasheet question, and the instrument for
                it is the command table.  Choosing between those two, rather than
                reaching for whichever tool is nearest, is what the debugging
                chapter was about.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-breakit" room="compressed">
    <p><term>Two one-line changes to a program that works, and a capture of each.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li>The point is not either failure on its own. It is that the two look <term>completely different on the wire</term> and <term>identical on the display</term> &#8212; which is what makes a scope worth reaching for, and what makes it not enough.</li>
        <li>Set up: AD2 back on SDA and SCL in oscilloscope mode, as you had it yesterday, and work on a <term>copy</term> of <c>helloDisplay.c</c> so your driver project stays intact.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>Do not let the prediction in the second task be skipped &#8212; it is the whole beat. Almost everyone predicts a broken trace for case 2.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-breakit-answer">
    <p><term>A perfect trace and a blank display.</term></p>
    <ul>
        <li><term>Case 1</term> is visible on the wire: nine clock pulses, SDA HIGH on the ninth, then a STOP. And the program <term>stops</term> after one transaction &#8212; which is why the reset button was needed.</li>
        <li><term>Case 2</term> shows nothing wrong at all. Every transaction is there, every address byte is 0xE0, every ninth pulse comes back LOW: the HT16K33 is powered, listening and <term>acknowledging every byte you send it</term>. It is just not driving anything, because nothing told its oscillator to run.</li>
        <li>So <term>the bus can be flawless and the device still do nothing</term>, and no amount of staring at SDA and SCL will tell you that.</li>
        <li>A scope answers <q>did my bytes arrive?</q>, completely. It cannot answer <q>were they the right bytes?</q> &#8212; that is a datasheet question, and the instrument for it is the command table.</li>
    </ul>
    <note>The two-question split is the takeaway to say out loud. It is the debugging chapter's "which tool, when", made physical.</note>
</slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-day10-hang">
    <p><term>Why case 1 needs the reset button.</term> With the address at 0x60 the program stops after one transaction rather than repeating four times a second &#8212; so arm the single sweep first, then press the Nucleo's black button.</p>
    <ul>
        <li><c>i2c1_byteWrite()</c> tests for a NACK <term>once</term>, a microsecond or so after setting START &#8212; about ninety microseconds before the device could have failed to answer. The test always passes.</li>
        <li>It then waits for the peripheral to say it is ready for data, and RM0490 &#167;23.4.9 says plainly: <term>the TXIS flag is not set when a NACK is received</term>. The wait never ends.</li>
        <li>The <em>hardware</em> is unaffected: the same paragraph of the manual says a STOP is sent automatically after a NACK, which is why the capture looks tidy. It is the <term>program</term> that is stuck.</li>
        <li>The Reference section has the flow the reference manual gives for doing this without the hole. Worth knowing before Lab 5.</li>
    </ul>
</slide>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-day10-next">
            <title>Part 10: Before Next Class</title>
            <p>
                Two programs, both of them counters, and the second one is
                deliberately the shape of Lab 5.
            </p>
            <activity xml:id="act-i2c-homework">
                <title>Homework: Counters on the Display</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Due at the start of next class.  First, housekeeping
                        that Lab 5 depends on: rename the pair you just finished
                        from <c>SevenSegPartial.c</c> and
                        <c>SevenSegPartial.h</c> to <c>SevenSeg.c</c> and
                        <c>SevenSeg.h</c>, change the <c>#include</c> to match,
                        and move both into your <c>mylib</c> folder alongside
                        <c>i2c.c</c> and <c>i2c.h</c>.  That is where Lab 5
                        expects to find them.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-hw-t1">
                    <statement><p>A 16-bit unsigned counter, incrementing once a
                    second on the display.  Write
                    <c>SevenSeg_number()</c> to do the conversion: it takes a
                    number and fills a buffer with the segment patterns for its
                    digits.  Four digits hold 0 to 9999, so decide what it should
                    do with a number that does not fit and say so in a
                    comment.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-hw-t2">
                    <statement><p>An 8-bit signed counter, −128 to 127.  You will
                    need a minus sign, which is one segment; if you want an
                    absolute value, <c>#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;</c> gives you
                    <c>abs()</c>.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-hw-t3">
                    <statement><p>A clock, <c>MM:SS</c>, counting up from zero.
                    This one needs the colon, so it needs you to have found
                    which bit in <c>display_buffer[4]</c> lights it — two
                    minutes with the buffer and a rebuild will
                    settle it.</p></statement>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="act-i2c-hw-t4">
                    <statement><p>Optional, and worth doing before Lab 5: drive
                    the update from a timer interrupt rather than
                    <c>delay_ms()</c>, so the counter keeps time while the main
                    loop does something else.</p></statement>
                </task>
            </activity>
            <p>
                Lab 5 is due Tuesday 10 February.  It asks for a modular ADC
                driver, an ADC reading taken on a timer interrupt rather than a
                delay, and a seven-segment display driver — and it says to use
                the code you developed in class today as the basis for the last
                of those.  The counters above are the same shape as what it
                wants on the display.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-i2c-reference">
        <title>Reference: The Protocol, the I2C Peripheral, and the HT16K33</title>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Lookup material for the two class days.  Nothing here is new;
                it is the same information arranged for the moment you come back
                needing one fact, plus the things the hours did not have room
                for.  It opens with the protocol in full — the written version of
                what the first of the two days works through on an
                oscilloscope — because that is the part you are most likely to
                come back to.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-physical-reality">
            <title>The Protocol: Two Wires, One Shared Bus</title>
            <p>
                I2C uses exactly two wires: <term>SCL</term>, the serial clock,
                and <term>SDA</term>, the serial data line.  Both run the length
                of the circuit, and every I2C device is connected to both — not
                by its own pair back to the microcontroller, but tapped onto the
                same two wires as everything else.  When one device puts a bit
                on SDA, every device sees it.
            </p>
            <p>
                A shared medium like that is called a <term>bus</term>, and what
                it buys is that the wire count does not grow.  A display, a
                temperature sensor and an accelerometer all connect to the same
                two wires; adding a fourth device adds no wires at all.
            </p>
            <p>
                Both lines idle HIGH, held there by pull-up resistors.  A device
                can pull a line LOW, and that is the only thing it can actively
                do: to send a 1 it releases the line and lets the resistor
                bring it up.  This is called <term>open-drain</term> signaling,
                and it is what makes a shared wire safe.  If two devices drive
                SDA at the same time, one pulling LOW and one releasing, nothing
                is damaged and nothing is ambiguous — the line goes LOW,
                because a pull-down always wins over a pull-up.  A device that
                released the line and then reads it LOW can tell that somebody
                else is holding it down, which turns out to be useful.
            </p>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-addressing">
            <title>The Protocol: Addressing, and the ACK</title>
            <p>
                Because every device hears every bit, the protocol needs a way
                to reach one device without disturbing the rest.  Every I2C
                device has a 7-bit <term>address</term>, fixed by its
                manufacturer and printed in its datasheet.  At the start of
                every transaction the controller broadcasts that address plus a
                single read/write bit — eight bits, clocked out one at a time on
                SDA — and every device on the bus compares the address it hears
                against its own.
            </p>
            <p>
                The device whose address matches answers by pulling SDA LOW
                during the ninth clock pulse.  That one-bit reply is the
                <term>ACK</term>, for acknowledge.  From the controller's side:
                SDA LOW after the address byte means a device recognized the
                address and the transaction can go on.  SDA staying HIGH is a
                <term>NACK</term> — nobody claimed that address, which usually
                means the address is wrong, the device is unpowered, or a wire
                is off.
            </p>
            <p>
                Data bytes follow the same way, each one acknowledged by
                whichever side is receiving: the device for a write, the
                controller for a read.  A NACK on a data byte tells the sender
                the receiver could not take it.
            </p>
            <p>
                One device is in charge of all of this.  The
                <term>controller</term> — our STM32C031C6 — generates the clock,
                starts every transaction and decides who is being addressed.
                Everything else on the bus is a <term>target</term>, and a
                target only ever speaks when spoken to.  (Older documents,
                including the reference manual's own I2C chapter, use
                <q>master</q> and <q>slave</q> for these two roles.  The
                hardware is the same; the words have changed.)
            </p>
        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-start-stop">
            <title>The Protocol: START and STOP Conditions</title>
            <p>
                SDA is allowed to change only while SCL is LOW.  That is when
                the receiver is not sampling it; while SCL is HIGH,
                SDA holds still and is read.  The protocol then takes the one
                thing that rule forbids and uses it to mark the edges of a
                transaction, which is why a START can never be mistaken for
                data.
            </p>
            <p>
                A <term>START condition</term> is SDA going from HIGH to LOW
                <em>while SCL is HIGH</em>.  It cannot occur during normal data
                transfer, so every device on the bus recognizes it immediately:
                a new transaction is beginning.
            </p>
            <p>
                A <term>STOP condition</term> is SDA going from LOW to HIGH
                while SCL is HIGH.  Also impossible as a data bit, it means the
                transaction is over and the bus is free.
            </p>
            <p>
                Both are in <xref ref="fig-i2c-frame-diagram"/>.  The SDA
                transitions at the START and STOP markers happen while SCL is
                HIGH; every other SDA edge in the diagram happens while SCL is
                LOW.  That contrast is the whole framing mechanism.
            </p>

        </subsection>
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-synchronous">
            <title>The Protocol: Why There Is No Baud Rate</title>
            <p>
                UART requires both ends to agree on a baud rate in advance
                (<xref ref="ch-uart"/>).  Nothing on the wire says when a bit
                begins; the receiver starts a timer at the start bit and samples
                on its own schedule.  Get the rate wrong at either end and the
                frame comes out as garbage.
            </p>
            <p>
                I2C removes that agreement by sending the clock.  The controller
                drives SCL, and everything on the bus reads SDA while SCL is
                HIGH.  No device has to know in advance how fast the bits will
                arrive, because the wire tells it.  That is what
                <term>synchronous</term> means here: the clock is shared, not
                reconstructed independently at each receiver.  I2C is
                synchronous; UART is asynchronous.
            </p>
            <p>
                The controller still picks a clock speed, and it has to pick one
                every device on the bus can follow.  The standard defines
                100 kHz (Standard mode), 400 kHz (Fast mode) and 1 MHz
                (Fast-mode Plus); the parts in this course run comfortably at
                100 kHz and that is what we use.  Adding a new device does not
                change the timing of any existing one.
            </p>
            <p>
                What synchronous costs is the extra wire for the clock, and the
                fact that the two wires are shared: only one device may talk at
                a time, so unlike UART — which has a separate line each way and
                can send and receive at once — I2C is half duplex.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-ref-registers">
            <title>Register Summary</title>
            <table xml:id="table-i2c-registers">
                <title>The registers this chapter uses</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <col width="19%"/>
                    <col width="20%"/>
                    <col width="48%"/>
                    <col width="13%"/>
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Register</cell>
                        <cell>Field(s) used</cell>
                        <cell>Purpose</cell>
                        <cell>Source</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>I2C_CR1</c></cell>
                        <cell>PE</cell>
                        <cell>Peripheral enable — clear it before writing TIMINGR, set it when done</cell>
                        <cell>RM §23.7.1</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>I2C_CR2</c></cell>
                        <cell>SADD, RD_WRN, NBYTES, AUTOEND, START</cell>
                        <cell>Describes one whole transaction: who, which direction, how many bytes, what to do at the end, and go</cell>
                        <cell>RM §23.7.2</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>I2C_TIMINGR</c></cell>
                        <cell>PRESC, SCLL, SCLH, SDADEL, SCLDEL</cell>
                        <cell>SCL timing — PRESC divides I2CCLK by PRESC+1; the other four are counted in units of the prescaled clock</cell>
                        <cell>RM §23.7.5</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>I2C_ISR</c></cell>
                        <cell>BUSY, TXIS, RXNE, NACKF</cell>
                        <cell>Status — a START is on the bus; ready for a byte to send; a byte has arrived; a NACK was received</cell>
                        <cell>RM §23.7.7</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>I2C_TXDR</c> / <c>I2C_RXDR</c></cell>
                        <cell>whole register</cell>
                        <cell>The byte going out, and the byte that came in</cell>
                        <cell>RM §23.7.10–11</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>GPIOx_MODER</c></cell>
                        <cell>MODE8, MODE9</cell>
                        <cell>Alternate function mode on PB8 and PB9</cell>
                        <cell>RM §6.4.1</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>GPIOx_OTYPER</c></cell>
                        <cell>OT8, OT9</cell>
                        <cell>Output type — 1 is open-drain, 0 (reset) is push-pull</cell>
                        <cell>RM §6.4.2</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>GPIOx_AFRH</c></cell>
                        <cell>AFSEL8, AFSEL9</cell>
                        <cell>Which alternate function — AF6 is I2C1 on both pins; written in C as <c>AFR[1]</c></cell>
                        <cell>DS Table 15</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell><c>RCC_APBENR1</c></cell>
                        <cell>I2C1EN</cell>
                        <cell>Enable the I2C1 peripheral clock</cell>
                        <cell>RM §5.4.13</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                <q>RM</q> is RM0490, the STM32C0x1 reference manual; <q>DS</q> is
                the STM32C031 datasheet.  The I2C chapter is §23 and the GPIO
                chapter is §6.  Note that the reference manual's I2C chapter
                still uses <q>master</q> and <q>slave</q> where this course says
                controller and target, so <c>SADD</c> is documented as the
                <q>slave address</q> field.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-ref-hardware">
            <title>What Is Under I2C1-&gt;CR2</title>
            <p>
                The library describes a transaction and reads flags.  Everything
                between that and the two wires is the peripheral, and
                <xref ref="fig-i2c-hardware"/> is the chip's own drawing of it.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-hardware">
                <caption>The I2C peripheral, from RM0490 Figure 211.  The
                registers along the bottom are what software touches; everything
                above them runs on its own.  Data control holds the shift
                register that turns a byte into eight bits on SDA and back;
                clock control generates SCL from the I2CCLK input and the timing
                register, and also contains the slave-clock-stretching logic; the
                analog and digital noise filters sit between the pins and the
                rest.  The block on the left is <em>wake-up on address match</em>
                — the feature that lets a device asleep in a low-power mode be
                woken by its own address arriving on the bus.  We are always the
                controller and never asleep, so we never use it, and address
                recognition proper would be <c>I2C_OAR1</c> and the <c>ADDR</c>
                flag rather than this block.  The SMBus blocks belong to a
                related protocol this course does not use
                either.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_hardware_block.svg" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Two things on it are worth knowing about even though we never
                configure them.  The <term>noise filters</term> are why a short
                spike on SDA does not become a bit: an analog filter is on by
                default and suppresses pulses up to 50 ns, which is what the I2C
                specification requires.  And the shift register is the reason
                <c>I2C_TXDR</c> is one byte wide rather than a buffer — the
                peripheral holds exactly one byte in hand while it clocks the
                previous one out, which is what <c>TXIS</c> is telling you.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-ref-cr2">
            <title>I2C_CR2, Field by Field</title>
            <p>
                One assignment to <c>I2C_CR2</c> describes an entire
                transaction, which is why it is the register the library writes
                most (<xref ref="fig-i2c-cr2"/>).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-cr2">
                <caption>I2C control register 2, from RM0490 §23.7.2, with the
                five fields the course's library writes boxed.  In the lower row,
                <c>SADD[9:0]</c> holds the address; <c>RD_WRN</c> is the transfer
                direction, 0 for a write and 1 for a read; and <c>START</c> is
                the bit whose being written is what puts a START condition on the
                wire.  In the upper row, <c>NBYTES[7:0]</c> is how many data
                bytes this transaction will carry, and <c>AUTOEND</c> makes the
                hardware issue the STOP by itself once they have gone.  The
                remaining fields — <c>NACK</c>, <c>HEAD10R</c>, <c>ADD10</c>,
                <c>RELOAD</c> and <c>PECBYTE</c> — belong to target mode, 10-bit
                addressing, transfers longer than 255 bytes, and SMBus, none of
                which this course uses.  <c>STOP</c> is the exception: it is a
                controller bit, and it is how software ends a transfer itself
                when <c>AUTOEND</c> is clear.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day09X-I2C/i2c_cr2.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <c>SADD</c> is the field with the trap in it.  The manual is
                exact: in 7-bit addressing mode, <q>SADD[7:1] must be written
                with the 7-bit slave address to be sent.  The bits SADD[9],
                SADD[8] and SADD[0] are don't care.</q>  The address goes in bits
                7 down to 1, which is what the <c>&lt;&lt;1</c> in the library is
                doing, and bit 0 — where the R/W bit will appear on the wire — is
                ignored here, because the direction comes from <c>RD_WRN</c>
                instead.
            </p>
            <p>
                <c>AUTOEND</c> is worth knowing about because of what the other
                setting is for.  With <c>AUTOEND</c> set, the hardware sends a
                STOP after <c>NBYTES</c> bytes and the bus goes idle.  With it
                clear, the peripheral instead holds SCL LOW and sets a
                <q>transfer complete</q> flag, which lets software start a second
                transfer without releasing the bus in between — a
                <term>repeated START</term>.  That is how a read from a device
                register works: write the register address, repeated START, then
                read, with no STOP in the middle where another controller could
                take the bus.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-ref-library">
            <title>The Library, and Where It Cuts Corners</title>
            <p>
                Reading one byte is the same shape as writing one, with
                <c>RD_WRN</c> set, a wait on <c>RXNE</c> instead of
                <c>TXIS</c>, and the data coming out of <c>I2C_RXDR</c> instead
                of going into <c>I2C_TXDR</c>:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
    // Ping target address for reading one byte
    I2C1->CR2 = (deviceAddr<<1)                  // target address
                | (1 << I2C_CR2_NBYTES_Pos)      // will read one byte
                | I2C_CR2_RD_WRN                 // ping for reading
                | I2C_CR2_AUTOEND                // Send Stop condition after transfer
                | I2C_CR2_START;                 // initiate start condition
    while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_BUSY) );        // Wait until Start is received

    // Read the data
    if( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_NACKF) ) {         // Make sure ACK received
        while( !(I2C1->ISR & I2C_ISR_RXNE) );    // Wait for Rx to be ready
        *data = I2C1->RXDR;                      // Read the data
    }
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                Both functions have the same hole in them, and the break-it
                exercise in class is where you meet it.  The <c>NACKF</c>
                test runs a microsecond or so after <c>START</c> is set, which is
                roughly ninety microseconds before the device would have had a
                chance to not answer — so the test always passes.  Execution then
                reaches <c>while( !(I2C1->ISR &amp; I2C_ISR_TXIS) );</c>, and
                RM0490 §23.4.9 says plainly that on a NACK <q>the TXIS flag is
                not set</q>.  The wait never ends.
            </p>
            <p>
                What the hardware does is unaffected — RM0490 §23.4.9 continues
                <q>a STOP condition is automatically sent after the NACK
                reception</q>, which is the peripheral's own NACK handling rather
                than <c>AUTOEND</c> completing a transfer — so the bus is
                released and the capture on the oscilloscope looks exactly as it
                should.  It is the program that stops, which
                is why pinging a wrong address gives you one transaction and then
                silence rather than four a second.
            </p>
            <p>
                RM0490's Figure 228 gives the flow that does not have the hole:
                a loop that tests <c>TXIS</c> and <c>NACKF</c> on every pass and
                leaves on whichever comes first.  Writing it that way costs a few
                lines and gives the caller a way to find out that nobody
                answered, which matters as soon as a program has to do something
                about a device that is not there.  Worth knowing when you build
                on this library rather than just using it.
            </p>
            <p>
                The narrower lesson is worth stating in general, because the
                NACK is not the only way to hit it: <term>none of the waits in
                this library has a timeout.</term>  Every
                <c>while( !(I2C1->ISR &amp; ...) );</c> in it assumes the hardware
                will eventually do what it was asked.  The very first one —
                waiting for <c>BUSY</c> after setting <c>START</c> — assumes SDA
                and SCL can rise at all, which they cannot if the bus has no
                pull-up anywhere on it (<xref ref="subsec-i2c-ref-opendrain"/>).
                A bus stuck LOW hangs the program on its <em>first</em> I2C call,
                before any of the NACK story applies.  Production code puts a
                bound on every one of these waits and reports the timeout to its
                caller; this library is a teaching library and does not.
            </p>
            <p>
                <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> and <c>i2c1_memRead()</c> follow the
                same pattern with a larger <c>NBYTES</c> and the register address
                sent first.  You are given both and only ever call them.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-ref-opendrain">
            <title>What Else Open-Drain Buys</title>
            <p>
                Part 6a gave one reason the I2C pins must be open-drain: SDA
                changes hands every ninth bit, and two push-pull outputs
                disagreeing about a wire is a short circuit.  Two more features
                of the protocol rest on the same property, and both are worth
                recognizing even though this course never sets either up.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Sizing the pull-up.</term>  Part 6a said the value is
                squeezed from both ends (<xref ref="fig-open-drain"/>); here is
                the arithmetic.  At the low end, the device pulling the line down
                has to sink the whole of 3.3 V ÷ R and still hold the line below
                the receiver's LOW threshold — 3.3 V through 1 kΩ is 3.3 mA,
                which most parts manage, and through 200 Ω is 16 mA, which many
                do not.  At the high end, nothing drives the line up: the
                resistor charges the bus's own capacitance, so the rise is an
                exponential with time constant <m>RC</m>, and it has to finish
                inside the clock's LOW phase.  With a few hundred picofarads of
                bus capacitance, 10 kΩ gives a time constant of a few
                microseconds, which is already a noticeable fraction of a 5 µs
                LOW phase at 100 kHz.  That is the whole of the 5–10 kΩ
                convention: small enough to be quick, large enough to be sinkable.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Arbitration.</term>  I2C allows more than one controller on
                a bus.  If two of them start a transaction at the same moment,
                each one watches SDA while it transmits.  A controller that
                releases the line — sending a 1 — and then reads it LOW knows
                that somebody else is pulling it down, which means somebody else
                is sending a 0 at a bit position where it sent a 1.  It has lost,
                and it stops transmitting immediately.  The other controller
                never notices anything happened.  This works only because the
                combined line reads LOW if <em>any</em> device pulls it LOW,
                which is the wired-AND behavior open-drain gives you for free.
            </p>
            <p>
                <term>Clock stretching.</term>  SCL is open-drain too, and a
                target that needs more time can hold it LOW after the controller
                has released it.  The controller's clock generator waits for the
                line to actually rise before starting to time the HIGH phase, so
                the whole bus slows down for as long as the target needs.  It is
                flow control with no protocol overhead at all — one device
                holding a wire down.  RM0490 lists it in the I2C peripheral's
                features, and the <c>NOSTRETCH</c> bit in <c>I2C_CR1</c> is how a
                target would turn it off.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-ref-transfer-pattern">
            <title>The Register Read/Write Pattern</title>
            <p>
                The HT16K33 is unusual in being almost stateless: you send it a
                command or a block of display data and it acts.  Most I2C parts
                are organized as a set of numbered registers instead, and their
                datasheets describe access to those registers in the four shapes
                of <xref ref="fig-i2c-transfer-pattern"/>.  It is worth reading
                now, because it is the pattern behind
                <c>i2c1_memWrite()</c> and <c>i2c1_memRead()</c>, and because
                every I2C sensor you meet later is documented this way.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-i2c-transfer-pattern">
                <caption>The four register-access transfers, as an I2C sensor's
                datasheet draws them, with the sequence written out underneath
                the two single-byte cases.  ST is the START and SP the STOP; SAD is the device
                address; SUB is the sub-address, meaning the register number
                inside the device; SAK is the target's acknowledge; SR is a
                repeated START; and MAK and NMAK are the controller's own
                acknowledge and final not-acknowledge when it is the one
                receiving.  A write is device address, register number, then data
                bytes — and the register number advances by itself for each
                further byte, which is exactly what the HT16K33's display data
                pointer does.  A read needs both directions in one transaction:
                address and register number going out, then a repeated START to
                turn the bus around without releasing it, then the device address
                again with the read bit, and the data coming back.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day13-I2C(3)/i2c_transfer_pattern.svg" width="95%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                The repeated START in the read case is the reason
                <c>AUTOEND</c> exists as a choice rather than always being on.  A
                STOP between the two halves would free the bus, and on a bus with
                a second controller on it the register pointer you just set could
                be changed by somebody else before you read from it.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-i2c-ref-ht16k33">
            <title>HT16K33 Quick Reference</title>
            <p>
                Everything the driver needs, in one place.  Page numbers are the
                HT16K33 datasheet's.
            </p>
            <table xml:id="table-ht16k33-commands">
                <title>HT16K33 commands (datasheet pp. 24–25)</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <col width="26%"/>
                    <col width="26%"/>
                    <col width="48%"/>
                    <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
                        <cell>Command</cell>
                        <cell>Byte</cell>
                        <cell>Options</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Display data address pointer</cell>
                        <cell><c>0b0000</c> A3 A2 A1 A0</cell>
                        <cell>Where the following data bytes start; 0x00 for the whole display</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>System setup</cell>
                        <cell><c>0b0010</c> X X X S</cell>
                        <cell>S = 1 turns the internal oscillator on; 0x21 in full</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Display setup</cell>
                        <cell><c>0b1000</c> X B1 B0 D</cell>
                        <cell>D = 1 is display on; B1 B0 is 00 steady, 01 at 2 Hz, 10 at 1 Hz, 11 at 0.5 Hz.  0x81 is on and steady</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Dimming set</cell>
                        <cell><c>0b1110</c> P3 P2 P1 P0</cell>
                        <cell>Duty cycle in sixteenths, 0000 for 1/16 up to 1111 for 16/16.  0xEF is full</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>
            <p>
                The display RAM is sixteen bytes, two per common line, of which
                we use the five even addresses 0x00, 0x02, 0x04, 0x06 and 0x08 —
                digit 1, digit 2, the colon, digit 3, digit 4.  The odd addresses
                take a 0.  Within a digit's byte the bits are
                <c>0b</c> DP <c>g f e d c b a</c>: bit 0 is segment <c>a</c> and
                bit 7 is the decimal point.
            </p>
            <p>
                Writing the display is one page write from address 0x00
                (datasheet p. 22): the pointer advances one address per byte
                received, so ten bytes in one transaction fill the whole thing.
                Two separate transactions do not work, because the pointer is set
                by the command byte at the start of a transaction and a STOP ends
                it.
            </p>
            <p>
                One oddity in the given solutions, so it does not look like a
                mistake: <c>SevenSeg_dim()</c> sends
                <c>HT16K33_DISPLAY_CMD | HT16K33_BRIGHT_CMD | (brightness &amp; 0xf)</c>,
                and the dimming command already contains every bit the display
                command has — <c>0b1110</c> covers <c>0b1000</c> — so the first
                term changes nothing.  The value that goes out is 0xE0 ORed with
                the brightness either way.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
